,"cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1k5","text":"59"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1k6","text":"21"}]}]},{"_key":"a1k7","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1k8","text":"72"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1k9","text":"1"}]}]},{"_key":"a1ka","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1kb","text":"73"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1kc","text":"4"}]}]},{"_key":"a1kd","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ke","text":"–"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1kf","text":"20"}]}]},{"_key":"a1kg","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1kh","text":"75"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ki","text":"17"}]}]},{"_key":"a1kj","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1kk","text":"–"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1kl","text":"18"}]}]},{"_key":"a1km","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1kn","text":"76"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ko","text":"23"}]}]},{"_key":"a1kp","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1kq","text":"84"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1kr","text":"21"}]}]},{"_key":"a1ks","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1kt","text":"85"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ku","text":"21"}]}]},{"_key":"a1kv","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1kw","text":"Total"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1kx","text":"1356 + 3052 = 4408 (19×232)"}]}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1l0","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1kz","text":"Two other grammatical forms of the word “Quran” occur in 12 verses. These include the word “Quranun” and the word “Quranahu.” One of these occurrences, in 13:31 refers to “another Quran” that cause the mountains to crumble. Another occurrence, in 41:44, refers to “a non-Arabic Quran.” These two occurrences, therefore, are excluded. Table 21 shows a list of the suras and verses where the word “Quran,” in all its grammatical forms, occurs."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a1l1","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l2","text":"A Strong Foundation"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1l4","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l3","text":"The Quran’s first verse, “In the Name of God, Most Gracious, Most Merciful,” known as Basmalah, consists of 19 Arabic letters. Its constituent words occur in the Quran consistently in multiples of 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a1lp","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l5","text":"Frequency of the Basmalah's Four Words"}]},"table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l6","text":"Word"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"monoPrimaryMediumTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l7","text":"Occurrences"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l8","text":"Multiple of 19"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"}],"rows":[{"_key":"a1l9","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1la","text":"The first word — \"Ism\" (Name)"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1lb","text":"19"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1lc","text":"19 × 1"}]}]},{"_key":"a1ld","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1le","text":"The second word — \"Allah\" (God)"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1lf","text":"2,698"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1lg","text":"19 × 142"}]}]},{"_key":"a1lh","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1li","text":"The third word — \"Al-Rahman\" (Most Gracious)"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1lj","text":"57"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1lk","text":"19 × 3"}]}]},{"_key":"a1ll","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1lm","text":"The fourth word — \"Al-Raheem\" (Most Merciful)"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ln","text":"114"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1lo","text":"19 × 6"}]}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1lr","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1lq","text":"Professor Cesar Majul looked at the gematrical value of more than 400 attributes of God, and found only four names whose gematrical values are multiples of 19:"}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a1m6","table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ls","text":"Divine Name"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"monoPrimaryMediumTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1lt","text":"Gematrical Value"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"}],"rows":[{"_key":"a1lu","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1lv","text":"1. \"Waahed\" (One)"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1lw","text":"19"}]}]},{"_key":"a1lx","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ly","text":"2. \"Zul Fadl Al-'Azim\" (Possessor of Infinite Grace)"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1lz","text":"2698"}]}]},{"_key":"a1m0","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1m1","text":"3. \"Majid\" (Glorious)"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1m2","text":"57"}]}]},{"_key":"a1m3","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1m4","text":"4. \"Jaami'\" (Summoner)"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1m5","text":"114"}]}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1m8","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1m7","text":"As noted above, the only Divine Names whose gematrical values are divisible by 19 correspond exactly to the frequencies of occurrence of the Basmalah’s four words. The four words of Basmalah, the frequencies of occurrence of those words, and the only four divine names whose gematrical values are divisible by 19, all share the same four numbers: 19, 2698, 57, and 114."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a1m9","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ma","text":"The Five Pillars of Islam"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a1mv","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1mc","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1mb","text":"Although the Quran provides numerous important commandments governing all aspects of our lives (see for example 17:22-38), five basic “pillars” have been traditionally emphasized. They are:"}]},{"_type":"appendixBadgeList","_key":"a1ms","ordered":false,"style":"badge","item":"none","content":"none","contentTag":"none","items":[{"_key":"a1md","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1me","text":"Shahaadah:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1mf","text":" Bearing witness that there is no other god besides God."}]}},{"_key":"a1mg","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1mh","text":"Salat:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1mi","text":" Observing five daily Contact Prayers."}]}},{"_key":"a1mj","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1mk","text":"Seyaam:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ml","text":" Fasting during the ninth month of the Islamic calendar (Ramadan)."}]}},{"_key":"a1mm","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1mn","text":"Zakat:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1mo","text":" Giving away 2.5% of one’s net income as a charity to specified people."}]}},{"_key":"a1mp","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1mq","text":"Hajj:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1mr","text":" Pilgrimage to Mecca once in a lifetime for those who can afford it."}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1mu","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1mt","text":"Like everything else in the Quran, these are mathematically structured."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a1n0","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1mx","style":"term","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1mw","text":"1. One God (Shahaadah)"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1mz","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1my","text":"As mentioned earlier, the word “ONE” that refers to God occurs in the Quran 19 times. The reference to God “ALONE” occurs 5 times, and the sum of the sura and verse numbers where we find these five occurrences is 361, 19×19."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a1oa","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n1","text":"Table 22: All Suras and Verses from First Occurrence of LAA ELAAHA ELLA HOO to the Last Occurrence"}]},"table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n2","text":"Sura No."}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"monoPrimaryMediumTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n3","text":"No. of Verses"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n4","text":"Sum of Verse #s"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n5","text":"Total"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"}],"rows":[{"_key":"a1n6","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n7","text":"2"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n8","text":"123"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n9","text":"27675"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1na","text":"27800"}]}]},{"_key":"a1nb","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1nc","text":"3"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1nd","text":"200"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ne","text":"20100"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1nf","text":"20303"}]}]},{"_key":"a1ng","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1nh","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ni","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1nj","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1nk","text":"—"}]}]},{"_key":"a1nl","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1nm","text":"9"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1nn","text":"127"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1no","text":"8128"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1np","text":"8264"}]}]},{"_key":"a1nq","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1nr","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ns","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1nt","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1nu","text":"—"}]}]},{"_key":"a1nv","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1nw","text":"72"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1nx","text":"28"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ny","text":"406"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1nz","text":"506"}]}]},{"_key":"a1o0","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1o1","text":"73"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1o2","text":"9"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1o3","text":"45"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1o4","text":"127"}]}]},{"_key":"a1o5","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1o6","text":"Total"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1o7","text":"5312"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1o8","text":"308490"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1o9","text":"316502 (19×16658)"}]}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1oc","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ob","text":"The “First Pillar of Islam” is stated in 3:18 as “LAA ELAAHA ELLA HOO” (There is no other god besides Him). This most important expression occurs in 19 suras. The first occurrence is in 2:163, and the last occurrence is in 73:9. Table 22 shows that the total of sura numbers, plus the number of verses between the first and last occurrences, plus the sum of these verse numbers is 316502, or 19×16658."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1oe","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1od","text":"Also, by adding the numbers of the 19 suras where LAA ELAAHA ELLA HOO occurs, plus the verse numbers where this crucial expression is found, plus the total number of occurrences (29), the Grand Total comes to 2128, or 19×112. The details are shown in Table 23."}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a1rc","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1of","text":"Table 23: List of All Occurrences of the Crucial Phrase \"LAA ELAAHA ELLA HOO\" (There is no other god besides Him)"}]},"table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1og","text":"No."}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"monoPrimaryMediumTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1oh","text":"Sura No."}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1oi","text":"Verses with Shahadah"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1oj","text":"Frequency of Shahadah"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"}],"rows":[{"_key":"a1ok","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ol","text":"1."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1om","text":"2"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1on","text":"163, 255"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1oo","text":"2"}]}]},{"_key":"a1op","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1oq","text":"2."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1or","text":"3"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1os","text":"2, 6, 18, 18"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ot","text":"4"}]}]},{"_key":"a1ou","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ov","text":"3."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ow","text":"4"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ox","text":"87"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1oy","text":"1"}]}]},{"_key":"a1oz","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p0","text":"4."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p1","text":"6"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p2","text":"102, 106"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p3","text":"2"}]}]},{"_key":"a1p4","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p5","text":"5."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p6","text":"7"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p7","text":"158"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p8","text":"1"}]}]},{"_key":"a1p9","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pa","text":"6."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pb","text":"9"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pc","text":"31"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pd","text":"1"}]}]},{"_key":"a1pe","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pf","text":"7."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pg","text":"11"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ph","text":"14"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pi","text":"1"}]}]},{"_key":"a1pj","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pk","text":"8."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pl","text":"13"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pm","text":"30"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pn","text":"1"}]}]},{"_key":"a1po","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pp","text":"9."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pq","text":"20"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pr","text":"8, 98"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ps","text":"2"}]}]},{"_key":"a1pt","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pu","text":"10."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pv","text":"23"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pw","text":"116"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1px","text":"1"}]}]},{"_key":"a1py","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1pz","text":"11."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1q0","text":"27"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1q1","text":"26"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1q2","text":"1"}]}]},{"_key":"a1q3","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1q4","text":"12."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1q5","text":"28"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1q6","text":"70, 88"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1q7","text":"2"}]}]},{"_key":"a1q8","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1q9","text":"13."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qa","text":"35"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qb","text":"3"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qc","text":"1"}]}]},{"_key":"a1qd","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qe","text":"14."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qf","text":"39"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qg","text":"6"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qh","text":"1"}]}]},{"_key":"a1qi","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qj","text":"15."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qk","text":"40"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ql","text":"3, 62, 65"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qm","text":"3"}]}]},{"_key":"a1qn","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qo","text":"16."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qp","text":"44"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qq","text":"8"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qr","text":"1"}]}]},{"_key":"a1qs","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qt","text":"17."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qu","text":"59"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qv","text":"22, 23"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qw","text":"2"}]}]},{"_key":"a1qx","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qy","text":"18."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1qz","text":"64"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r0","text":"13"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r1","text":"1"}]}]},{"_key":"a1r2","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r3","text":"19."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r4","text":"73"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r5","text":"9"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r6","text":"1"}]}]},{"_key":"a1r7","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r8","text":"Total"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r9","text":"507"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ra","text":"1592"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1rb","text":"29"}]}]}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1rd","tone":"primary","style":"highlightCentered","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1rf","style":"primary","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1re","text":"507 + 1592 + 29 = 2128 = 19×112"}]}]},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a1ry","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1rh","style":"term","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1rg","text":"2. The Contact Prayers (Salat)"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1rj","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ri","text":"The word “Salat” occurs in the Quran 67 times, and when we add the numbers of suras and verses of these 67 occurrences, the total comes to 4674, or 19×246 (see INDEX OF THE QURAN)."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1rl","style":"term","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1rk","text":"3. Fasting (Seyaam)"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1rn","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1rm","text":"The commandment to fast is mentioned in 2:183, 184, 185, 187, 196; 4:92; 5:89, 95; 33:35, 35; & 58:4. The total of these numbers is 1387, or 19×73. It is noteworthy that 33:35 mentions fasting twice, one for the believing men, and the other for the believing women."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1rp","style":"term","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ro","text":"4. The Obligatory Charity (Zakat) & 5. Hajj Pilgrimage to Mecca"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1rr","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1rq","text":"While the first three “Pillars of Islam” are obligatory upon all Muslim men and women, the Zakat and Hajj are decreed only for those who can afford them. This explains the interesting mathematical phenomenon associated with Zakat and Hajj."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1rt","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1rs","text":"The Zakat charity is mentioned in 2:43, 83, 110, 177, 277; 4:77, 162; 5:12, 55; 7:156; 9:5, 11, 18, 71; 18:81; 19:13, 31, 55; 21:73; 22:41, 78; 23:4; 24:37, 56; 27:3; 30:39; 31:4; 33:33; 41:7; 58:13; 73:20; and 98:5. These numbers add up to 2395. This total does not quite make it as a multiple of 19; it is up by 1."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1rv","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ru","text":"The Hajj Pilgrimage occurs in 2:189, 196, 197; 9:3; and 22:27. These numbers add up to 645, and this total does not quite make it as a multiple of 19; it is down by 1."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1rx","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1rw","text":"Thus, Zakat and Hajj, together, give a total of 2395 + 645 = 3040 = 19×160."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a1rz","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1s0","text":"The Quran’s Mathematical Structure"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1s2","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1s1","text":"The Quran’s suras, verses, words, and letters are not only mathematically composed, but also arranged into a superhuman structure that is purely mathematical, i.e., the literary content has nothing to do with such an arrangement."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1s4","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1s3","text":"Since the physical construction of the Quran is purely mathematical, it would be expected that the numbers mentioned in the Quran must conform with the Quran’s 19-based code."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1s6","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1s5","text":"A total of 30 unique numbers are mentioned throughout the Quran, and the sum of all these numbers is 162146, a multiple of 19 (162146 = 19×8534). Table 24 lists all the numbers mentioned in the Quran, without the repetitions."}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a1uv","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1s7","text":"Table 24: All the Quranic Numbers"}]},"table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1s8","text":"Number"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"monoPrimaryMediumTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1s9","text":"Location Example"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"}],"rows":[{"_key":"a1sa","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1sb","text":"1"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1sc","text":"2:163"}]}]},{"_key":"a1sd","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1se","text":"2"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1sf","text":"4:11"}]}]},{"_key":"a1sg","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1sh","text":"3"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1si","text":"4:171"}]}]},{"_key":"a1sj","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1sk","text":"4"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1sl","text":"9:2"}]}]},{"_key":"a1sm","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1sn","text":"5"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1so","text":"18:22"}]}]},{"_key":"a1sp","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1sq","text":"6"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1sr","text":"25:59"}]}]},{"_key":"a1ss","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1st","text":"7"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1su","text":"41:12"}]}]},{"_key":"a1sv","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1sw","text":"8"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1sx","text":"69:17"}]}]},{"_key":"a1sy","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1sz","text":"9"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1t0","text":"27:48"}]}]},{"_key":"a1t1","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1t2","text":"10"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1t3","text":"2:196"}]}]},{"_key":"a1t4","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1t5","text":"11"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1t6","text":"12:4"}]}]},{"_key":"a1t7","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1t8","text":"12"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1t9","text":"9:36"}]}]},{"_key":"a1ta","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1tb","text":"19"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1tc","text":"74:30"}]}]},{"_key":"a1td","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1te","text":"20"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1tf","text":"8:65"}]}]},{"_key":"a1tg","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1th","text":"30"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ti","text":"7:142"}]}]},{"_key":"a1tj","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1tk","text":"40"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1tl","text":"7:142"}]}]},{"_key":"a1tm","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1tn","text":"50"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1to","text":"29:14"}]}]},{"_key":"a1tp","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1tq","text":"60"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1tr","text":"58:4"}]}]},{"_key":"a1ts","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1tt","text":"70"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1tu","text":"9:80"}]}]},{"_key":"a1tv","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1tw","text":"80"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1tx","text":"24:4"}]}]},{"_key":"a1ty","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1tz","text":"99"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1u0","text":"38:23"}]}]},{"_key":"a1u1","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1u2","text":"100"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1u3","text":"2:259"}]}]},{"_key":"a1u4","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1u5","text":"200"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1u6","text":"8:65"}]}]},{"_key":"a1u7","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1u8","text":"300"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1u9","text":"18:25"}]}]},{"_key":"a1ua","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ub","text":"1000"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1uc","text":"2:96"}]}]},{"_key":"a1ud","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ue","text":"2000"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1uf","text":"8:66"}]}]},{"_key":"a1ug","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1uh","text":"3000"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ui","text":"3:124"}]}]},{"_key":"a1uj","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1uk","text":"5000"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ul","text":"3:125"}]}]},{"_key":"a1um","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1un","text":"50000"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1uo","text":"70:4"}]}]},{"_key":"a1up","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1uq","text":"100000"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ur","text":"37:147"}]}]},{"_key":"a1us","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ut","text":"Total"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1uu","text":"162146 (19 × 8534)"}]}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1ux","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1uw","text":"The numbers which are mentioned only once in the Quran are: 11, 19, 20, 50, 60, 80, 99, 300, 2000, 3000, 5000, 50000, and 100000."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1uz","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1uy","text":"All the numbers mentioned in the Quran, with repetitions, occur 285 times, and this number is a multiple of 19; 285 = 19×15."}]},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a1v6","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1v1","style":"term","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1v0","text":"The Numbers of Suras and Verses"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1v3","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1v2","text":"The numbering system of the Quran’s suras and verses has been perfectly preserved. Only a few unauthorized and easily detectable printings deviate from the standard system that is divinely guarded."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1v5","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1v4","text":"When we add the numbers of all suras, plus the number of verses in every sura, plus the sum of verse numbers, the Grand total for the whole Quran comes to 346199, 19×19×959. Table 25 is an abbreviated presentation of these data. Thus, the slightest alteration of a single sura or verse would have destroyed this system. As shown in Table 16, if we consider only the 29 initialed suras, these same data produce a Grand Total which is also a multiple of 19. It follows that the data for the un-initialed suras are also divisible by 19. Table 26 is an abbreviated presentation of the same data related to the 85 un-initialed suras."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a1wg","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1v7","text":"Table 25: Mathematical Coding of the Sura and Verse Numbers"}]},"table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1v8","text":"Sura No."}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"monoPrimaryMediumTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1v9","text":"No. of Verses"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1va","text":"Sum of Verse #s"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vb","text":"TOTAL"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"}],"rows":[{"_key":"a1vc","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vd","text":"1"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ve","text":"7"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vf","text":"28"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vg","text":"36"}]}]},{"_key":"a1vh","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vi","text":"2"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vj","text":"286"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vk","text":"41041"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vl","text":"41329"}]}]},{"_key":"a1vm","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vn","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vo","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vp","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vq","text":"—"}]}]},{"_key":"a1vr","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vs","text":"9"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vt","text":"127"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vu","text":"8128"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vv","text":"8264"}]}]},{"_key":"a1vw","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vx","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vy","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1vz","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1w0","text":"—"}]}]},{"_key":"a1w1","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1w2","text":"113"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1w3","text":"5"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1w4","text":"15"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1w5","text":"133"}]}]},{"_key":"a1w6","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1w7","text":"114"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1w8","text":"6"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1w9","text":"21"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wa","text":"141"}]}]},{"_key":"a1wb","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wc","text":"Total"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wd","text":"6234"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1we","text":"333410"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wf","text":"346199 (19×19×959)"}]}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a1xq","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wh","text":"Table 26: Mathematical Coding of the 85 Un-initialed Suras"}]},"table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wi","text":"Sura No."}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"monoPrimaryMediumTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wj","text":"No. of Verses"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wk","text":"Sum of Verse #s"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wl","text":"TOTAL"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"}],"rows":[{"_key":"a1wm","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wn","text":"1"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wo","text":"7"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wp","text":"28"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wq","text":"36"}]}]},{"_key":"a1wr","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ws","text":"4"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wt","text":"176"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wu","text":"15576"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wv","text":"15756"}]}]},{"_key":"a1ww","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wx","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wy","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1wz","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x0","text":"—"}]}]},{"_key":"a1x1","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x2","text":"9"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x3","text":"127"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x4","text":"8128"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x5","text":"8264"}]}]},{"_key":"a1x6","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x7","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x8","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x9","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xa","text":"—"}]}]},{"_key":"a1xb","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xc","text":"113"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xd","text":"5"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xe","text":"15"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xf","text":"133"}]}]},{"_key":"a1xg","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xh","text":"114"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xi","text":"6"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xj","text":"21"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xk","text":"141"}]}]},{"_key":"a1xl","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xm","text":"Total"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xn","text":"3491"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xo","text":"146842"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xp","text":"156066 (19×8214)"}]}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a1xr","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xs","text":"Superhuman Numerical Combinations"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1xu","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xt","text":"Let us write down the number of each verse in the Quran, preceded for each sura by the number of verses in that sura. Thus, Sura 1, which consists of seven verses, will be represented by the number 7 1234567. What we are doing here is forming long numbers by writing the numbers of verses next to each other. To find the number representing Sura 2, you write down the number of verses in this sura, 286, followed by the number of every verse, written next to each other. Thus, the number representing Sura 2 will look like this: 286 12345.....284285286. The two numbers representing the first two suras are:"}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a1xv","style":"block","text":"block","value":"7 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 & 286 1 2 3 4 5 ..... 284 285 286"},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1xy","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1xx","text":"Putting these two numbers together to form one number representing the first two suras, we get this number:"}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a1xz","style":"block","text":"block","value":"7 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 286 1 2 3 4 5 ..... 284 285 286"},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1y2","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1y1","text":"This process is continued until every verse in the Quran is written down, thus forming one very long number encompassing the number of every verse in the Quran. The number representing the whole Quran is a multiple of 19 & consists of 12692 digits, which is also a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1y3","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1y6","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1y4","text":"First No.:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1y5","text":" This very long number consists of 12692 digits (19×668) and includes every verse in the Quran. The number of verses in each sura precedes its verses. A special computer program that divides very long numbers has shown that this long number is a multiple of 19."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1y8","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1y7","text":"Instead of putting the total number of verses in every sura ahead of the sura, let us put it at the end of every sura. Since we are putting the total number of verses per sura at the end of each sura, we must put the total number of numbered verses (6234) at the end of the Quran. Putting together all the verses of all the suras, produces a long number that consists of 12696 digits, and is a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a1y9","style":"block","text":"block","value":"1234567 7 12345...286 286 12345 5...123456 6 6234"},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1yb","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1ye","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1yc","text":"Second No.:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1yd","text":" The number of every verse in every sura is followed by the number of verses per sura. The last 11 digits shown here are the 6 verses of the last sura, followed by its number of verses (6), followed by the number of numbered verses in the Quran (6234). The complete, very long number, is a multiple of 19."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1yg","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1yf","text":"Now let us include the number of every sura. Write down the number of every verse in every sura, followed by the number of the sura, followed by the number of verses in the sura. The total number of numbered verses (6234) is added at the end. This number, representing the whole Quran, is a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a1yh","style":"block","text":"block","value":"1234567 1 7 12345...286 2 286 ...123456 114 6 6234"},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1yj","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1ym","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1yk","text":"Third No.:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1yl","text":" The number of every verse, followed by the sura number, then the number of verses in the sura. The total number of numbered verses is added at the end. The long number (12930 digits) is a multiple of 19."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1yo","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1yn","text":"Instead of putting the total number of verses in every sura after the sura, let us now put it ahead of the sura. This very long number representing the whole Quran is a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a1yp","style":"block","text":"block","value":"7 1234567 1 286 12345...286 2...6 123456 114 6234"},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1yr","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1yu","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1ys","text":"Fourth No.:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1yt","text":" The total number of verses in each sura is followed by the number of every verse, then the sura number. The last 14 digits shown above are the number of verses in the last sura (6), followed by the numbers of the six verses (123456), followed by the number of the sura (114), then the total number of numbered verses in the Quran. The very long number (consisting of 12930 digits) is a multiple of 19."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1yw","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1yv","text":"Now, let us write down the number of every verse in every sura, followed by the sum of verse numbers for every sura. Sura 1 consists of 7 verses, and the sum of verse numbers is 1+2+3+4+5+6+7 = 28. The complete number, representing the whole Quran, consists of 12836 digits and is a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a1yx","style":"block","text":"block","value":"1234567 28 12345...284285286 41041...123456 21"},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1yz","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1z2","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1z0","text":"Fifth No.:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1z1","text":" The number of every verse in every sura is followed by the sum of verse numbers. The long number consists of 12836 digits, and is a multiple of 19."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1z4","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1z3","text":"Remarkably, if we take the “Fifth No.” shown above and reverse the order of verse numbers and sum of verse numbers, i.e., move the sum of verse numbers, and put it ahead of the sura, the resulting long number is still a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a1z5","style":"block","text":"block","value":"28 1234567 41041 12345....285286.....21 123456"},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1z7","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1za","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1z8","text":"Sixth No.:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1z9","text":" Placing the sum of verse numbers ahead of each sura, instead of after it, produces a long number (12836 digits) that is also a multiple of 19."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1zc","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1zb","text":"Even writing the suras backward, i.e., reversing the order of suras by starting with the last sura and ending with the first sura, and placing the sum of verse numbers after the verses of each sura, the product is still a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a1zd","style":"block","text":"block","value":"123456 21 12345 15..12345..286 41041 1234567 28"},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1zf","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1zi","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1zg","text":"Seventh No.:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1zh","text":" Reversing the order of suras — starting from the last sura and ending with the first sura — and writing down the number of every verse, with the sum of verse numbers for every sura after its verses, the product is a long number consisting of 12836 digits. This long number is a multiple of 19."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1zk","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1zj","text":"Write the sum of verse numbers for the whole Quran (333410), followed by the number of numbered verses in the Quran (6234), then the number of suras (114). Every sura is then represented by its number followed by its number of verses. The complete number, covering all suras of the Quran, consists of 474 digits, and is a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a1zl","style":"block","text":"block","value":"333410 6234 114 1 7 2 286 3 200..113 5 114 6"},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1zn","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1zq","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1zo","text":"Eighth No.:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1zp","text":" The Grand Sum of verse numbers (333410) is followed by the number of numbered verses (6234), the number of suras (114), then the sura numbers and numbers of verses of every sura."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1zs","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1zr","text":"Now let us reverse the order of sura number and its number of verses as presented in the “Eighth No.” The complete number also consists of 474 digits and is still a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a1zt","style":"block","text":"block","value":"333410 6234 114 7 1 286 2 200 3...5 113 6 114"},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1zv","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1zy","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1zw","text":"Ninth No.:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1zx","text":" Reversing the sequence of sura number and number of verses still gives us a long number that is a multiple of 19."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a200","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1zz","text":"If we write down the sum of verse numbers for Sura 1 (28), followed by the sum of verse numbers for Sura 2 (41041), and so on to the end of the Quran, and placing the Grand Sum of verse numbers (333410) at the end, the resulting long number (Tenth No.) consists of 377 digits, and is a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a201","style":"block","text":"block","value":"28 41041 20100 ..... 15 21 333410"},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a203","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a206","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a204","text":"Tenth No.:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a205","text":" The sums of verse numbers for every sura in the Quran, are written next to each other, followed at the end by the Grand Sum of verse numbers (333410). This long number (377 digits) is a multiple of 19."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a208","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a207","text":"If we write down the number of suras in the Quran (114), followed by the total number of numbered verses (6234), followed by the number of every sura and its sum of verse numbers, the final long number (612 digits) is a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a209","style":"block","text":"block","value":"114 6234 1 28 2 41041 3 20100...113 15 114 21"},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a20b","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a20e","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a20c","text":"Eleventh No.:","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a20d","text":" The number of suras, followed by the number of numbered verses, then the number of every sura and its sum of verse numbers, produce this long number (612 digits) that is a multiple of 19."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a20g","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a20f","text":"Lest anyone may think that any Quranic parameter is left un-guarded with this awesome mathematical code, let us look at more parameters. If we write down the number of suras (114), followed by the number of numbered verses, followed by the Grand Sum of verse numbers in the whole Quran (333410), followed by the numbers of every sura and its verses, we end up with a very long number (12712 digits) that is a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a20h","style":"block","text":"block","value":"114 6234 333410 1 1 2 3 4 5 6 7...114 1 2 3 4 5 6"},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a20j","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a20l","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a20k","text":"Twelfth Number.","marks":["term"]}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a20n","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a20m","text":"If we write down the numbers of verses in every sura next to each other, we end up with a 235-digit number that is a multiple of 19. To do this, write down the total number of numbered verses in the Quran (6234), followed by the number of verses in every sura, then close with the total number of numbered verses in the Quran."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a20o","style":"block","text":"block","value":"6234 | 7 286 200 176 | .... | 127 | .... | 5 4 5 6 | 6234"},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a20q","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a20t","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a20r","text":"Thirteenth Number.","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a20s","text":" If we write down the number of numbered verses in the Quran (6234), followed by the number of suras (114), followed by the number of every verse in every sura, then close with the number of verses in the Quran (6234) and the number of suras (114), the final number consists of 12479 digits, and is a multiple of 19."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a20u","style":"block","text":"block","value":"6234 114 1234567 12345...286...123456 6234 114"},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a20w","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a20z","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a20x","text":"Fourteenth Number.","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a20y","text":" Another long number that consists of 12774 digits is formed by writing down the number of every verse in every sura, followed by the number of every sura added to its number of verses. Sura 1 consists of 7 verses, and the total 1+7 is 8. Since Sura 2 consists of 286 verses, the number representing Sura 2 looks like this: 12345...286 288. This is done for every sura in the Quran. The final combined number is a multiple of 19."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a210","style":"block","text":"block","value":"1234567 8 12345 ... 286 288 ........... 123456 120"},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a212","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a215","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a213","text":"Fifteenth Number.","marks":["term"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a214","text":" More specialized features are in Appendices 2, 9, 19, 24, 25, 26, 29, and 37."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a216","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a217","text":"Last Minute Discovery"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a21j","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a219","style":"muted","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a218","text":"[May 26, 1989 — Add to Appendix 1]"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a21b","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21a","text":"Mr. Abdullah Arik made the following discoveries just before printing time:"}]},{"_type":"appendixBadgeList","_key":"a21i","ordered":true,"style":"decimal","item":"none","content":"none","contentTag":"none","items":[{"_key":"a21c","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21d","text":"If we write down the number of verses in every sura, followed by the sum of verse numbers, and keep all number justified to the left, the total of all left-justified numbers is 4,859,309,774, or 19×255753146 (Table 1)."}]}},{"_key":"a21e","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21f","text":"Do the same as above, except write down the number of every verse, instead of the number of verses. The total this time consists of 757 digits and is still a multiple of 19 (Table 2)."}]}},{"_key":"a21g","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21h","text":"Do the same as above, but justify everything to the right, and write down the sura number, followed by the number of verses in that sura, followed by the number of every verse in the sura, and finally, the sum of verse numbers. The total consists of 759 digits and is also a multiple of 19 (Table 3)."}]}}]}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a21k","tone":"primary","style":"highlightCentered","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a21m","style":"eyebrowPrimarySm","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21l","text":"God Be Glorified."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a225","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21n","text":"Table 1"}]},"table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21o","text":"Sura"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"monoPrimaryMediumTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21p","text":"Number"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"}],"rows":[{"_key":"a21q","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21r","text":"Sura 1"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21s","text":"728"}]}]},{"_key":"a21t","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21u","text":"Sura 2"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21v","text":"28641041"}]}]},{"_key":"a21w","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21x","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21y","text":"—"}]}]},{"_key":"a21z","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a220","text":"Sura 114"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a221","text":"621"}]}]},{"_key":"a222","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a223","text":"Total"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a224","text":"4859309774 = 19 × 255753146"}]}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a22l","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a226","text":"Table 2"}]},"table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a227","text":"Sura"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"monoPrimaryMediumTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a228","text":"Number"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"}],"rows":[{"_key":"a229","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22a","text":"Sura 1"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22b","text":"123456728"}]}]},{"_key":"a22c","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22d","text":"Sura 2"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22e","text":"1234...28641041"}]}]},{"_key":"a22f","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22g","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22h","text":"—"}]}]},{"_key":"a22i","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22j","text":"Sura 114"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22k","text":"12345621"}]}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a231","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22m","text":"Table 3"}]},"table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22n","text":"Sura"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"monoPrimaryMediumTop"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22o","text":"Number"}]},"headerStyle":"leftSemibold","cellStyle":"plainTop"}],"rows":[{"_key":"a22p","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22q","text":"Sura 1"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22r","text":"17123456728"}]}]},{"_key":"a22s","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22t","text":"Sura 2"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22u","text":"2286123 ... 28641041"}]}]},{"_key":"a22v","style":"dataEmphasised","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22w","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22x","text":"—"}]}]},{"_key":"a22y","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22z","text":"Sura 114"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a230","text":"114612345621"}]}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a232","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a233","text":"A Witness From the Children of Israel [46:10]"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a234","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a236","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a235","text":"Proclaim: “What if it is from God, and you disbelieved in it? A witness from the Children of Israel has borne witness to a similar phenomenon, and he has believed, while you have turned too arrogant to believe. God does not guide the wicked.” [46:10]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a238","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a237","text":"The following quotation is taken from STUDIES IN JEWISH MYSTICISM (Association for Jewish Studies, Cambridge, Mass., Joseph Dan & Frank Talmage, eds., Page 88, 1982). The quotation refers to the work of Rabbi Judah the Pious (12th Century AD):"}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a239","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a23b","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a23a","text":"The people [Jews] in France made it a custom to add [in the morning prayer] the words: “ ‘Ashrei temimei derekh [blessed are those who walk the righteous way],” and our Rabbi, the Pious, of blessed memory, wrote that they were completely and utterly wrong. It is all gross falsehood, because there are only nineteen times that the Holy Name is mentioned [in that portion of the morning prayer]... and similarly you find the word ‘Elohim nineteen times in the pericope of Ve-‘elleh shemot. . . . Similarly, you find that Israel were called “sons” nineteen times, and there are many other examples. All these sets of nineteen are intricately intertwined, and they contain many secrets and esoteric meanings, which are contained in more than eight large volumes. . . Furthermore, in this section there are 152 (19×8) words. ..."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a23c","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a23d","text":"Acknowledgments"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"block","_key":"a23f","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a23e","text":"All praise and thanks are due to God who has willed that His miracle of the Quran shall be revealed at this time. He has distinguished the following individuals and blessed them by revealing through them many portions of this momentous discovery: Abdullah Arik, Mohamoud Ali Abib, Lisa Spray, Edip Yuksel, Ihsan Ramadan, Feroz Karmally, Ismail Barakat, Gatut Adisoma, Ahmed Yusuf (of Lagos), Cesar A. Majul, Muhtesem Erisen, Emily Kay Sterrett and Cecilia Albertha Wallen."}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a23g","tone":"muted","style":"attributionStacked","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a23i","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a23h","text":"United Submitters International / International Community of Submitters / Masjid Tucson"}]}],"reveal":false}]$pt$::jsonb, NULL, NULL),

-- Appendix 2: God's Messenger of the Covenant
('2', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a4","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“God took a covenant from the prophets, saying, ‘After I have given you the scripture and wisdom, a messenger will come to confirm what you have. You shall believe in him and support him.’ He said, ‘Do you agree with this, and pledge to uphold this covenant?’ They said, ‘We agree.’ He said, ‘You have thus borne witness, and I am with you a witness.’”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"3:81"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a6","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"God's Messenger of the Covenant is a consolidating messenger. His mission is to purify and unify all existing religions into one: Submission (Islam). Islam is NOT a name; it is a description of one's total submission and devotion to God ALONE, without idolizing Jesus, Mary, Muhammad, or the saints. Anyone meeting this criterion qualifies as a “Muslim” (Submitter) — Muslim Jews, Muslim Christians, and so on."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ac","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":"The Quran is clear that the only religion approved by God is Submission ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8","reference":"3:19"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":"), and that anyone who seeks other than Submission as a religion, it will not be accepted ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"3:85"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ag","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":"All previous messengers required divine proof of their messengership: Moses transformed his staff into a serpent, Jesus healed lepers and revived the dead, Saleh produced the famous camel, Abraham walked out of fire, and Muhammad's proof was the Quran itself ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ae","reference":"29:50-51"},{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":"). A new messenger likewise requires divine, incontrovertible proof."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"ah","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ai","text":"A Quranic Truth"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"al","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aj","reference":"33:7"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ak","text":": “And when we exacted a covenant from the Prophets, and from thee (O Muhammad) and from Noah and Abraham and Moses and Jesus son of Mary, We took from them a solemn covenant.”"}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"am","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"an","text":"Prophet vs. Messenger"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"at","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ao","text":"The Quran makes a critical distinction between "},{"_type":"span","_key":"ap","text":"Nabi","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"aq","text":" (Prophet) and "},{"_type":"span","_key":"ar","text":"Rasoul","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"as","text":" (Messenger). A prophet delivers a new scripture, while a messenger confirms existing scripture without bringing new revelation. Every prophet is also a messenger, but not every messenger is a prophet."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a13","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"au","text":"Not every messenger received a new scripture. Some assume Aaron was a prophet ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"av","reference":"19:53"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aw","text":") who was given no scripture, yet the Quran states the Torah was given “to both Moses and Aaron” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ax","reference":"21:48"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ay","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"az","reference":"37:117"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a10","text":"). It has also always been a human trait to reject a living messenger: Joseph was even declared “the last messenger” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a11","reference":"40:34"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a12","text":"), though Moses, David, Solomon, Jesus, and Muhammad all came after him."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a16","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a14","reference":"33:40"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":" states: “Muhammad was not the father of any of your men; he was a messenger (Rasoul) of God and the last prophet (Nabi).” Muhammad was thus the last prophet — no new scripture after him — but messengers can and do come after him. The gematrical value of “Muhammad Khaatum Al-Nabiyyeen” (the last prophet), plus the sura number (33) and the verse number (40), equals 1349, or 19×71. By contrast, the value of the erroneous expression “Muhammad Khaatum Al-Mursaleen” (the last messenger) is not a multiple of 19 — confirming the distinction through the Quran's mathematical code."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a17","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a18","text":"The Prophecy"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1g","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a19","text":"References to a consolidating messenger appear throughout the Quran at "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1a","reference":"3:81"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1b","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1c","reference":"33:7"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1d","text":", and "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1e","reference":"33:40"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1f","text":". This prophecy is also found in the Bible at Malachi 3:1–3: “Lo, I am sending my messenger to prepare the way before me; and suddenly there will come to the temple the Lord whom you seek and the messenger of the covenant whom you desire.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1l","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1h","text":"The covenant was confirmed in a personal experience during the Hajj pilgrimage on Zul-Hijjah 3, 1391 (December 21, 1971): “I was sitting still, while the prophets, one by one, came towards me, looked at my face, then nodded their heads.” Notably, the numerical sum of month (12) + day (3) + year (1391) = 1406 = 19×74. The number 19 is mentioned in Sura 74. The number 1406 is also the count of years from the revelation of the Quran to the revelation of its miracle ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1j","text":"Appendix 1","marks":["a1i"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1k","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"a1i","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/1"}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a1m","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n","text":"The Proof"}]}},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a2c","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1p","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1o","text":"The name of God's Messenger of the Covenant is mathematically coded into the Quran as “Rashad Khalifa” — the most appropriate way to introduce God's messenger to the world in the computer age. The following is physical, examinable, verifiable, and irrefutable evidence."}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1q","tone":"primary","style":"example","content":[{"_type":"appendixBadgeList","_key":"a2b","ordered":false,"style":"bulletBody","item":"none","content":"none","contentTag":"none","items":[{"_key":"a1r","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1s","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1t","text":"(1)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1u","text":" The Quran's 19-based miracle remained hidden for 1406 years (19×74), predestined to be unveiled through Rashad Khalifa. For fourteen centuries, hundreds of Muslim and Orientalist scholars tried in vain to decipher the Quranic Initials."}]}},{"_key":"a1v","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1w","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x","text":"(2)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1y","text":" The Quran is made easy only for sincere believers ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1z","reference":"54:17"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a20","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a21","reference":"39:28"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a22","text":"). No one is granted access to the Quran — let alone its miracle — without specific divine authorization ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a23","reference":"17:45-46"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a24","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a25","reference":"18:57"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a26","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a27","reference":"41:44"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a28","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a29","reference":"56:79"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2a","text":"). Its unveiling through Rashad Khalifa is a major sign of his messengership."}]}}]}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a4t","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2d","text":"Table 1: Suras and Verses of “Rashada” and “Khalifa”"}]},"table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2i","text":"#"}]},"headerStyle":"leftMono","cellStyle":"monoMutedAlt"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2j","text":"Sura"}]},"headerStyle":"left","cellStyle":"monoPrimaryMedium"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2k","text":"Verse"}]},"headerStyle":"left","cellStyle":"monoAlt"}],"rows":[{"_key":"a2l","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2m","text":"1"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2n","text":"2"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2o","text":"186"}]}]},{"_key":"a2p","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2q","text":"2"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2r","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2s","text":"256"}]}]},{"_key":"a2t","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2u","text":"3"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2v","text":"4"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2w","text":"6"}]}]},{"_key":"a2x","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2y","text":"4"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2z","text":"7"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a30","text":"146"}]}]},{"_key":"a31","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a32","text":"5"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a33","text":"11"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a34","text":"78"}]}]},{"_key":"a35","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a36","text":"6"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a37","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a38","text":"87"}]}]},{"_key":"a39","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3a","text":"7"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3b","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3c","text":"97"}]}]},{"_key":"a3d","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3e","text":"8"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3f","text":"18"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3g","text":"10"}]}]},{"_key":"a3h","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3i","text":"9"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3j","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3k","text":"17"}]}]},{"_key":"a3l","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3m","text":"10"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3n","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3o","text":"24"}]}]},{"_key":"a3p","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3q","text":"11"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3r","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3s","text":"66"}]}]},{"_key":"a3t","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3u","text":"12"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3v","text":"21"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3w","text":"51"}]}]},{"_key":"a3x","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3y","text":"13"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3z","text":"40"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a40","text":"29"}]}]},{"_key":"a41","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a42","text":"14"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a43","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a44","text":"38"}]}]},{"_key":"a45","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a46","text":"15"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a47","text":"49"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a48","text":"7"}]}]},{"_key":"a49","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4a","text":"16"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4b","text":"72"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4c","text":"2"}]}]},{"_key":"a4d","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4e","text":"17"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4f","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4g","text":"10"}]}]},{"_key":"a4h","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4i","text":"18"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4j","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4k","text":"14"}]}]},{"_key":"a4l","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4m","text":"19"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4n","text":"—"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4o","text":"21"}]}]},{"_key":"a4p","style":"totalsMuted","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4q","text":"Totals"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4r","text":"224"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4s","text":"1145"}]}],"cellStyles":["plain","monoPrimary","monoAlt"]}],"note":{"style":"plain","inside":true,"content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a2f","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2e","text":"Repeated sura numbers are shown as “—” and counted once: the nine distinct suras sum to 224, and the nineteen verse numbers sum to 1145."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a2h","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2g","text":"“Khalifa” occurs at 2:30 (a non-human khalifa — Satan) and 38:26 (a human khalifa). Sura 2 is already counted above, so “Khalifa” adds sura 38 and verses 30 + 26 = 56. Grand total: 224 + 1145 + 38 + 56 = 1463 = 19×77."}]}]}},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a6k","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a4u","tone":"primary","style":"example","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a4w","style":"term","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4v","text":"Numbered Mathematical Proofs"}]},{"_type":"appendixBadgeList","_key":"a65","ordered":false,"style":"bulletBody","item":"none","content":"none","contentTag":"none","items":[{"_key":"a4x","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4y","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a4z","text":"(3)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a50","text":" The root word “Rashada” (to uphold the right guidance) occurs in the Quran exactly 19 times — the Quran's common denominator."}]}},{"_key":"a51","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a52","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a53","text":"(4)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a54","text":" “Rashad” occurs at 40:29 and 40:38. “Khalifa” occurs at 2:30 (a non-human khalifa — Satan) and 38:26 (a human khalifa). Adding the suras and verses of “Rashad” (40:29, 38) and the human “Khalifa” (38:26): 40+29+38+38+26 = 171 = 19×9."}]}},{"_key":"a55","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a56","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a57","text":"(5)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a58","text":" The sum of all sura and verse numbers where “Rashada” and “Khalifa” occur, combined = 1463 = 19×77."}]}},{"_key":"a59","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5a","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5b","text":"(6)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5c","text":" “Rashada” total = 1369 = (19×72) + 1; “Khalifa” total = 94 = (19×5) − 1. “Rashada” up by one and “Khalifa” down by one pins the name to “Rashad Khalifa” — not any other Rashad or Khalifa."}]}},{"_key":"a5d","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5e","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5f","text":"(7)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5g","text":" Gematrical value of “Rashad” = 505; “Khalifa” = 725 (combined 1230). From the beginning of the Quran to the first “Rashada” (2:186): 3 suras, 192 verses. 505 + 725 + 3 + 192 = 1425 = 19×75."}]}},{"_key":"a5h","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5i","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5j","text":"(8)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5k","text":" The sum of all verse numbers from 1:1 through 2:186 (first “Rashada”) = 17,233 = 19×907."}]}},{"_key":"a5l","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5m","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5n","text":"(9)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5o","text":" The Quranic Initials appear in 29 suras; the sum of those sura numbers = 822. 822 + 1230 (Rashad Khalifa) = 2052 = 19×108."}]}},{"_key":"a5p","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5q","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5r","text":"(10)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5s","text":" The sura numbers plus their verse counts for all suras containing “Rashada” = 1368 = 19×72."}]}},{"_key":"a5t","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5u","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5v","text":"(11)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5w","text":" Writing each sura number, followed by its number of verses, followed by the individual verse numbers, from the first “Rashada” (2:186) to the last (72:21), produces a single number of 11,087 digits that is divisible by 19."}]}},{"_key":"a5x","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5y","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5z","text":"(12)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a60","text":" From the first “Rashada” to the word “Khalifa” in 38:26, the sum of the sura numbers and their verse counts = 4541 = 19×239."}]}},{"_key":"a61","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a62","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a63","text":"(13)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a64","text":" Writing the value of “Rashad” (505), then “Khalifa” (725), then every sura and verse number where “Rashada” occurs from 2:186 to “Khalifa” (38:26), produces a number divisible by 19."}]}}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a68","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a66","text":"(14)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a67","text":" The Quran specifies three messengers of Submission (Islam): Abraham, who delivered all the practices of Islam (gematrical value 258); Muhammad, who delivered the Quran (92); and Rashad, who delivered Islam's proof of authenticity (505). Total: 258 + 92 + 505 = 855 = 19×45."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a6g","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a69","text":"The true Judaism, Christianity, and Islam will be consolidated into one religion — complete submission and absolute devotion to God ALONE. The existing religions are severely corrupted and will die out ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6a","reference":"9:33"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a6b","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6c","reference":"48:28"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a6d","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6e","reference":"61:9"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a6f","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a6j","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6h","text":"(15)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a6i","text":" Since the Quran sometimes refers to “Abraham, Ismail, and Isaac,” the gematrical values of all five messengers were added — and the total remains a multiple of 19:"}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a7c","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6l","text":"Gematrical Values of the Five Messengers"}]},"table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6m","text":"Name"}]},"headerStyle":"left","cellStyle":"mediumSm"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6n","text":"Letter Values"}]},"headerStyle":"left","cellStyle":"monoMutedAlt"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6o","text":"Total"}]},"headerStyle":"leftMono","cellStyle":"monoPrimaryMedium"}],"rows":[{"_key":"a6p","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6q","text":"Abraham"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6r","text":"1+2+200+5+10+40"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6s","text":"258"}]}]},{"_key":"a6t","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6u","text":"Ismail"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6v","text":"1+60+40+70+10+30"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6w","text":"211"}]}]},{"_key":"a6x","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6y","text":"Isaac"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6z","text":"1+60+8+100"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a70","text":"169"}]}]},{"_key":"a71","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a72","text":"Muhammad"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a73","text":"40+8+40+4"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a74","text":"92"}]}]},{"_key":"a75","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a76","text":"Rashad"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a77","text":"200+300+1+4"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a78","text":"505"}]}]},{"_key":"a79","style":"totalsMuted","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7a","text":"Total"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7b","text":"1235 = 19×65"}]}],"cellStyles":["plain","monoPrimary"]}]},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a84","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a7e","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7d","text":"This divisibility by 19 is not possible if any of the three names Abraham, Muhammad, or Rashad is omitted."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a7h","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7f","text":"Verse 3:81","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7g","text":" carries profound mathematical confirmation:"}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a7i","tone":"primary","style":"example","content":[{"_type":"appendixBadgeList","_key":"a83","ordered":false,"style":"bulletBody","item":"none","content":"none","contentTag":"none","items":[{"_key":"a7j","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7k","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7l","text":"(16)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7m","text":" 505 (Rashad) + 725 (Khalifa) + 81 (verse number) = 1311 = 19×69."}]}},{"_key":"a7n","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7o","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7p","text":"(17)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7q","text":" Sura 81, verse 19 mentions a messenger “powerfully supported and authorized.”"}]}},{"_key":"a7r","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7s","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7t","text":"(18)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7u","text":" From 1:1 to 3:81: Sura 1 (7 verses) + Sura 2 (286 verses) + Sura 3 (81 verses) = 374 verses. 1+2+3+7+286+81 = 380 = 19×20."}]}},{"_key":"a7v","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7w","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7x","text":"(19)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7y","text":" The gematrical value of the entire verse 3:81 = 13,148 = 19×692."}]}},{"_key":"a7z","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a80","text":"• "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a81","text":"(20)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a82","text":" The key phrase “JAA'AKUM RASOOLUN MUSADDIQUN LEMAA MA'AKUM” (a messenger will come to confirm what you have) = 836 = 19×44."}]}}]}]}]},{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a88","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a85","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a86","text":"“Surely, you are one of the messengers.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a87","reference":"36:3"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a8b","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a89","text":"(21)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8a","text":" Among the initialed suras, Sura 36 (Ya Seen) occupies position number 19. The identity of the messenger in 36:3 was revealed through the angel Gabriel."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a8e","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a8c","text":"(22)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8d","text":" The gematrical value of verse 36:3 is 612. Calculation: 36 + 3 + 612 + 505 + 725 = 1881 = 19×99."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a8h","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a8f","text":"(23)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8g","text":" Sura 36 has 83 verses: 36 + 83 + 505 + 725 = 1349 = 19×71."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a8k","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a8i","text":"(24)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8j","text":" From 3:81 to Sura 36 there are 3330 verses. 505 + 725 + 3330 = 4560 = 19×240."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a8n","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a8l","text":"(25)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8m","text":" From 3:81 to 36:3 there are exactly 3333 verses. 3333 + 505 = 3838 = 19×202."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a8q","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a8o","text":"(26)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8p","text":" From 1:1 to 36:3 there are 3705 verses = 19×195."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a8t","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a8r","text":"(27)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8s","text":" The sum of all verse numbers from 1:1 to 36:3 = 257,925 = 19×13,575."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a8w","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a8u","text":"(28)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8v","text":" The sum of sura numbers 1 through 36 = 666. Calculation: 666 + 505 + 725 + 612 = 2508 = 19×132."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a8z","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a8x","text":"(29–30)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8y","text":" From the first “Rashada” (2:186) to 36:3, the sum of sura numbers = 665 = 19×35, representing 35 suras. The full sum of sura totals + verse numbers = 241,395 = 19×12,705."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a90","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a91","text":"A Messenger to the People of the Scripture"}]}},{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a95","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a92","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a93","text":"“O people of the scripture, our messenger has come to you, to clarify things for you, after a long period without messengers. Lest you say, ‘No preacher or warner has come to us.’ A preacher and warner has come to you. God is Omnipotent.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a94","reference":"5:19"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a98","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a96","text":"(31)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a97","text":" The verse number is 19 — the Quran's common denominator and the number of “Rashada” occurrences."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a9b","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a99","text":"(32)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9a","text":" 1230 (Rashad Khalifa) + 5 (sura) + 19 (verse) = 1254 = 19×66."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a9e","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9c","text":"(33)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9d","text":" From the beginning of the Quran through 5:19: Sura 1 (7 verses) + Sura 2 (286) + Sura 3 (200) + Sura 4 (176) + Sura 5 (19) = 688 verses total; 1+2+3+4+5+7+286+200+176+19 = 703 = 19×37."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a9f","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9g","text":"The Proof (Sura 98)"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"a9j","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a9h","reference":"98:1-2"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9i","text":": “Those who disbelieved among the people of the scripture, and the idolators, will not believe, despite the profound sign given to them. A messenger from God, reciting Sacred Scriptures.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a9m","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9k","text":"(34)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9l","text":" 505 (Rashad) + 725 (Khalifa) + 98 (sura) + 2 (verse) = 1330 = 19×70."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a9p","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9n","text":"(35)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9o","text":" The word “Bayyinah” (profound sign), the title of Sura 98, occurs in the Quran exactly 19 times — confirming that the Quran's proof is based on 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a9q","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9r","text":"A Profound Messenger Has Come"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"a9u","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9s","text":"(36)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9t","text":" From 1:1 to 44:13: the sum of sura numbers = 990, verse numbers = 4425. Combined: 5415 = 19×19×15."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a9x","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9v","text":"(37)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9w","text":" Sura number (44) + verse number (13) = 57 = 19×3."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a9y","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9z","text":"End of the World"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"aad","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aa0","text":"God alone knows the end of the world ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa1","reference":"7:187"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aa2","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa3","reference":"31:34"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aa4","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa5","reference":"33:63"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aa6","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa7","reference":"41:47"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aa8","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa9","reference":"43:85"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aaa","text":"), yet He chooses to reveal certain knowledge to messengers He selects ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aab","reference":"72:27"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aac","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"aag","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aae","text":"(38–39)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"aaf","text":" Total verses from the beginning of the Quran to 72:27 = 5472 = 19×72×4. Sura 72 contains four occurrences of “Rashada” at verses 2, 10, 14, and 21. The sum 1230+72+2+10+14+21 = 1349 = 19×71."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"aal","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aah","text":"(40)","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"aai","text":" The opening phrase of "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aaj","reference":"72:27"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aak","text":", “Only the Messenger that He chooses,” has a gematrical value of 1919."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"aam","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aan","text":"Mercy from God"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"aav","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aao","text":"When the believers are faced with a problem, they develop a number of possible solutions, and this invariably leads to considerable bickering, disunity, and disarray. It is but mercy from God that He sends to us messengers to provide the final solutions to our problems ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aap","reference":"2:151"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aaq","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aar","reference":"3:164"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aas","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aat","reference":"21:107"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aau","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ab3","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aaw","text":"God sends His messengers to communicate with us, and to disseminate new information ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aax","reference":"42:51"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aay","text":"). We are enjoined to accept, without the slightest hesitation, the teachings delivered to us through God's messengers ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aaz","reference":"4:65"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab0","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ab1","reference":"4:80"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab2","text":")."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"ab4","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ab5","text":"Criteria for God's Messenger"}]}},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"ac5","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"ab7","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ab6","text":"Three minimum criteria identify God's Messenger of the Covenant:"}]},{"_type":"appendixBadgeList","_key":"abe","ordered":true,"style":"badgeSm","item":"badge","marker":"primary","content":"sm","contentTag":"span","items":[{"_key":"ab8","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ab9","text":"God's messenger advocates the worship of God ALONE, and the abolition of all forms of idol worship."}]}},{"_key":"aba","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"abb","text":"God's messenger never asks for a wage for himself."}]}},{"_key":"abc","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"abd","text":"God's messenger is given divine, incontrovertible proof of his messengership."}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ac2","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"abf","text":"The most important difference between God's messenger and a fake messenger is that God's messenger is supported by God, while the fake messenger is not. God's messenger is supported by God's invisible soldiers ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"abg","reference":"3:124-126"},{"_type":"span","_key":"abh","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"abi","reference":"9:26"},{"_type":"span","_key":"abj","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"abk","reference":"9:40"},{"_type":"span","_key":"abl","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"abm","reference":"33:9"},{"_type":"span","_key":"abn","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"abo","reference":"37:171-173"},{"_type":"span","_key":"abp","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"abq","reference":"48:4"},{"_type":"span","_key":"abr","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"abs","reference":"48:7"},{"_type":"span","_key":"abt","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"abu","reference":"74:31"},{"_type":"span","_key":"abv","text":"), by God's treasury ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"abw","reference":"63:7-8"},{"_type":"span","_key":"abx","text":"), and is guaranteed victory and dignity, in this world and forever ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aby","reference":"40:51"},{"_type":"span","_key":"abz","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac0","reference":"58:21"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ac1","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ac4","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ac3","text":"Thus, the truthfulness of God's messenger invariably prevails, while the falsehood of a fake messenger invariably, sooner or later, is exposed."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"ac6","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ac7","text":"Principal Duties"}]}},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"aee","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"ac9","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ac8","text":"The principal duties of God's Messenger of the Covenant include:"}]},{"_type":"appendixBadgeList","_key":"aed","ordered":true,"style":"badge","item":"badge","marker":"primary","content":"sm","contentTag":"span","items":[{"_key":"aca","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"acb","text":"Unveil the Quran's mathematical miracle ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"acd","text":"Appendix 1","marks":["acc"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"ace","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"acc","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/1"}]}},{"_key":"acf","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"acg","text":"Remove the false verses 9:128–129 ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"aci","text":"Appendix 24","marks":["ach"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"acj","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"ach","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/24"}]}},{"_key":"ack","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"acl","text":"Explain the purpose of our life ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"acn","text":"Appendix 7","marks":["acm"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"aco","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"acm","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/7"}]}},{"_key":"acp","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"acq","text":"Proclaim one religion and purge corruptions from Judaism, Christianity, and Islam ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"acs","text":"Appendix 13","marks":["acr"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"act","text":", "},{"_type":"span","_key":"acv","text":"15","marks":["acu"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"acw","text":", "},{"_type":"span","_key":"acy","text":"19","marks":["acx"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"acz","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"acr","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/13"},{"_key":"acu","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/15"},{"_key":"acx","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/19"}]}},{"_key":"ad0","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ad1","text":"Proclaim Zakat as a prerequisite for redemption ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ad2","reference":"7:156"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad3","text":") and explain its correct practice ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad5","text":"Appendix 15","marks":["ad4"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad6","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"ad4","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/15"}]}},{"_key":"ad7","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ad8","text":"Unveil the end of the world ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"ada","text":"Appendix 25","marks":["ad9"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"adb","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"ad9","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/25"}]}},{"_key":"adc","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"add","text":"Proclaim that those who die before the age of 40 are destined for Heaven ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"adf","text":"Appendix 32","marks":["ade"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"adg","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"ade","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/32"}]}},{"_key":"adh","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"adi","text":"Explain the death of Jesus ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"adk","text":"Appendix 22","marks":["adj"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"adl","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"adj","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/22"}]}},{"_key":"adm","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"adn","text":"Explain the delivery of the Quran through Muhammad ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"adp","text":"Appendix 28","marks":["ado"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"adq","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"ado","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/28"}]}},{"_key":"adr","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ads","text":"Announce that Muhammad wrote the Quran with his own hand ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"adu","text":"Appendix 28","marks":["adt"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"adv","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"adt","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/28"}]}},{"_key":"adw","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"adx","text":"Explain why most believers do not reach Heaven ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"adz","text":"Appendix 27","marks":["ady"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"ae0","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"ady","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/27"}]}},{"_key":"ae1","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ae2","text":"Proclaim that God never ordered Abraham to kill his son ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"ae4","text":"Appendix 9","marks":["ae3"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"ae5","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"ae3","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/9"}]}},{"_key":"ae6","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ae7","text":"Proclaim the secret of perfect happiness (Introduction)."}]}},{"_key":"ae8","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ae9","text":"Establish the Quran's criminal justice system ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"aeb","text":"Appendix 37","marks":["aea"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"aec","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"aea","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/37"}]}}]}]}]$pt$::jsonb, NULL, NULL),

-- Appendix 3: We Made the Quran Easy
('3', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"aa","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“We made the Quran easy to learn. Does anyone wish to learn?”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"54:17"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a4","text":" · also "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a5","reference":"54:22"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a6","text":" · "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a7","reference":"54:32"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8","text":" · "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a9","reference":"54:40"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ae","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":"Verse "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac","reference":"11:1"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":" informs us that the Quran’s Miracle involves [1] the superhuman mathematical design of its physical structure and [2] the simultaneous composition of a literary work of extraordinary excellence."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ag","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":"One may be able to meet the numerical distribution requirements of a simple mathematical pattern. However, this is invariably accomplished at the expense of the literary quality. The simultaneous control of the literary style and the intricate mathematical distribution of individual letters throughout the Quran (Appendix 1) is evident in the fact that the Quran is made easy to memorize, understand, and enjoy. Unlike a human-made book, the Quran is enjoyable to read over and over, infinitely."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ai","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":"The title of this Appendix is repeated in Sura 54, verses 17, 22, 32, and 40. As it turns out, the Quran’s Arabic text is composed in such a way as to remind the reader or the memorizer of the next correct expression, or the next verse. God created us and He knows the most efficient way for fixing literary materials into our memory. Memorization of the Quran has played a vital role in preserving the original text generation after generation at a time when written books were a rarity."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ak","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aj","text":"Without even realizing it, the person who memorizes the Quran is divinely helped by an intricate literary system as he utters the sounds of the Quranic words. Almost every verse in the Quran contains what I call “Memory Bells.” Their function is to remind the reader of what comes next. This system is so vast, I will give only two illustrative examples:"}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"al","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"am","text":"Memory Bells"}]}},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"an","tone":"primary","style":"example","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"ap","style":"term","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ao","text":"Example 1"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ar","style":"body","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aq","text":"In Sura 2, Verses 127, 128, and 129 end with two different names of God each. These pairs of names are “Al-Samee‘ Al-‘Aleem (The Hearer, the Omniscient),” “Al-Tawwaab Al-Raheem (The Redeemer, Most Merciful)”, and “Al-‘Azeez Al-Hakeem (The Almighty, Most Wise),” respectively. If this were a regular book, one would easily mis-match these six names. Not so in the Quran. Each one of these pairs is preceded in the same verse by a “Memory Bell” that reminds us of the correct pair of names. Thus, Verse 127 talks about Abraham and Ismail raising the foundations of the Ka‘abah. The verse ends with the names “Al-Samee‘ Al-‘Aleem.” The prominent sounds here are the “S,” “M,” and “ ‘Ayn.” These three letters are prominent in the word “Ismail.” We find that this word is conspicuously delayed in the sentence, while improving its literary quality. Thus, we find that the verse goes like this: “When Abraham raised the foundations of the Ka‘abah, together with Ismail ...” Normally, a human writer would say, “When Abraham and Ismail raised the foundations of the Ka‘abah....” But delaying the sounds in “Ismail” brings them closer to the end of the verse, and thus reminds us that the correct names of God in this verse are “Al-Samee‘ Al-‘Aleem.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"av","style":"body","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"as","text":"Verse 128 has the prominent word “Tubb” just before the names “Al-Tawwaab Al-Raheem.” The word “Tubb” thus serves as the memory bell. The names of God at the end of "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"at","reference":"2:129"},{"_type":"span","_key":"au","text":" are “Azeez, Hakeem.” The prominent sounds here are “Z” and “K.” Obviously, the memory bell in this verse is the word “Yuzakkeehim.”"}]}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"aw","tone":"primary","style":"example","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"ay","style":"term","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":"Example 2"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a16","style":"body","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"az","text":"Another good example is found in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a10","reference":"3:176"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a11","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a12","reference":"3:177"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a13","text":", & "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a14","reference":"3:178"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":", where the retribution for disbelievers is described as “ ‘Azeem (Terrible),” “Aleem (Painful),” and “Muheen (Humiliating),” respectively. In a human-made book, the memorizer could easily mix up these three descriptions. But we find that each of these adjectives is preceded by powerful memory bells that prevent such a mix-up. The word “ ‘Azeem” of Verse 176 is preceded by the word “Huzzun,” which is characterized by a stressed letter “Z.” This serves to remind us of the particular adjective at the end of this verse. The word “Aleem” of Verse 177 is preceded by the sound of the word “Iman” to serve as a memory bell, and the word “Muheen” of 3:178 is preceded by an abundance of “M” and “H” throughout this verse."}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1o","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":"Other examples of memory bells include the ending of "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a18","reference":"3:173"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a19","text":" and the beginning of "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1a","reference":"3:174"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1b","text":", the ending of "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1c","reference":"4:52"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1d","text":" and the beginning of "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1e","reference":"4:53"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1f","text":", the ending of "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1g","reference":"4:61"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1h","text":" and the beginning of "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1i","reference":"4:62"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1j","text":", the ending of "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1k","reference":"18:53"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l","text":" and the beginning of "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1m","reference":"18:54"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n","text":", and many more."}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1q","tone":"muted","style":"attribution","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1s","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r","text":"United Submitters International / International Community of Submitters / Masjid Tucson"}]}],"reveal":false}]$pt$::jsonb, 'PwY2nPtuNVE', 'Appendix 3 — We Made the Quran Easy'),

-- Appendix 4: Why Was the Quran Revealed in Arabic?
('4', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a4","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“If we revealed this to people who do not know Arabic. And had him recite it (in Arabic), they could not possibly believe in it.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"26:198-199"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ag","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"We learn from "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"41:44"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":" that the sincere believers have access to the Quran, regardless of their mother tongue. The disbelievers, on the other hand, are not permitted access to the Quran, even if they are professors of the Arabic language ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8","reference":"17:45"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"18:57"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac","reference":"41:44"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ae","reference":"56:79"},{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ai","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":"Arabic is the most efficient language in the world, especially when it comes to the precise statement of laws. Since the Quran is a Statute Book, it was crucial that such laws be clearly stated. God chose Arabic for His Final Testament because it is the most suitable language for that purpose."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"aj","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ak","text":"The Efficiency of Arabic"}]}},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a1f","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"am","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"al","text":"Arabic is unique in its efficiency and accuracy. For example, the word “they” in English does not tell you if “they” are males or females. Arabic has four distinct forms:"}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"an","tone":"primary","style":"example","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"ap","style":"term","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ao","text":"Pronouns for “They” in Arabic"}]},{"_type":"appendixBadgeList","_key":"a12","ordered":false,"style":"bulletBody","item":"none","content":"none","contentTag":"none","items":[{"_key":"aq","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ar","text":"HUM","marks":["monoStrong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"as","text":" — they (males)"}]}},{"_key":"at","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"au","text":"HUNNA","marks":["monoStrong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"av","text":" — they (females)"}]}},{"_key":"aw","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":"HUMAA","marks":["monoStrong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"ay","text":" — they (two males)"}]}},{"_key":"az","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a10","text":"HAATAAN","marks":["monoStrong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a11","text":" — they (two females)"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a14","style":"mutedXs","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a13","text":"This feature does not exist in any other language in the world."}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a18","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":"This precision was extremely helpful in stating laws such as in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a16","reference":"2:228"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":", which enjoins the divorcee to give up her own wishes to divorce her husband if she discovers she is pregnant and the husband wishes to reconcile — the welfare of the child takes priority. The efficiency of the Arabic language made it possible to state this law clearly in very few words, specifying exactly whose wishes are to be superseded. Any other language would have made this almost impossible to convey with such brevity."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1e","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a19","text":"Another example is the word "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1a","text":"Qaalataa","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1b","text":" of "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1c","reference":"28:23"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1d","text":", which translates into four English words: “the two women said.” Such is the efficiency of Arabic."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a1g","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1h","text":"Gender and the Reference to God"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1j","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1i","text":"Another reason for choosing Arabic is that “He” and “She” in Arabic do not necessarily imply natural gender. Thus, when God is referred to as “He,” this does not imply gender at all. God be glorified; He is neither male nor female. The usage of “He” to refer to God in English, for example, has contributed to a false image of God — compounded further by distorted expressions such as “Father” when referring to God. Such a reference to God is never found in the Quran."}]}]$pt$::jsonb, 'BkZJa7j0z2M', 'Appendix 4 — Why Was the Quran Revealed in Arabic?'),

-- Appendix 5: Heaven and Hell
('5', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a4","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“Whoever succeeds in barely missing Hell, and is admitted into Heaven, has attained a great triumph.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"3:185"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ae","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"The descriptions of Heaven and Hell throughout the Quran are allegorical. And the Quran tells us so whenever such descriptions occur as independent statements, not within a general subject. See "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"2:24-26"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8","reference":"13:35"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":", and "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"47:15"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":". The word "},{"_type":"span","_key":"ac","text":"Mathal","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":" (allegory) is used in these verses. Linguistically, the word “Mathal” in these verses can be removed, and we still have perfect sentences. But it is there because the descriptions of Heaven and Hell are allegorical."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ag","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":"What Heaven and Hell are really like is far beyond our comprehension. Hence the need for allegory."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ai","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":"How can one describe, for example, the taste of chocolate to a person who never tasted chocolate? Allegory will have to be used. The person has to wait to actually taste chocolate in order to know what chocolate tastes like. Whatever allegory we use to describe the taste of chocolate can never approximate the real thing."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"aj","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ak","text":"Heaven and Hell"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"ar","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"al","text":"Heaven already exists, since Adam and Eve were placed in it during their days of innocence ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"am","reference":"2:35"},{"_type":"span","_key":"an","text":"). We learn from Sura 55 that there are two “High Heavens” - one for the humans and one for the jinns - and two “Lower Heavens” - one for the humans and one for the jinns (see "},{"_type":"span","_key":"ap","text":"Appendix 11","marks":["ao"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"aq","text":" for more details)."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"ao","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/11"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a10","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"as","text":"Hell is not created yet. It will be created on the Day of Judgment ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"at","reference":"69:17"},{"_type":"span","_key":"au","text":" & "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"av","reference":"89:23"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aw","text":"). More details are given in "},{"_type":"span","_key":"ay","text":"Appendix 11","marks":["ax"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"az","text":"."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"ax","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/11"}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a11","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a12","text":"The High Heaven vs The Lower Heaven"}]}},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a24","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a14","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a13","text":"There are profound differences between the High Heaven and the Lower Heaven."}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a15","tone":"primary","style":"exampleWide","content":[{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a1r","style":"grid","tag":"div","content":[{"_type":"appendixDefinitionRow","_key":"a16","term":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":"Water"}]},"termStyle":"term","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a18","text":"Allegorically, water in the High Heaven flows freely ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a19","reference":"55:50"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1a","text":"), while the water of the Lower Heaven needs to be pumped out ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1b","reference":"55:66"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1c","text":")."}]},"bodyStyle":"body"},{"_type":"appendixDefinitionRow","_key":"a1d","term":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1e","text":"Fruit"}]},"termStyle":"term","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1f","text":"Allegorically, the High Heaven has all kinds of fruit ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1g","reference":"55:52"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1h","text":"), while the Lower Heaven has a limited variety of fruits ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1i","reference":"55:68"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1j","text":")."}]},"bodyStyle":"body"},{"_type":"appendixDefinitionRow","_key":"a1k","term":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l","text":"Spouses"}]},"termStyle":"term","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1m","text":"Allegorically, the pure spouses readily join their spouses in the High Heaven ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1n","reference":"55:56"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1o","text":"), while the dwellers of the Lower Heaven must go fetch their spouses ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1p","reference":"55:72"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1q","text":")."}]},"bodyStyle":"body"}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a23","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1s","text":"Yet, even the Lower Heaven is an incredibly fantastic prize for those who are fortunate enough to escape Hell and end up in the Lower Heaven ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1t","reference":"3:185"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1u","text":") - going to the Lower Heaven is a great triumph. People who depart this life before reaching their 40th birthday, and did not sufficiently develop their souls, will go to the Lower Heaven ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1v","reference":"46:15"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1w","text":", "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1y","text":"Appendices 11","marks":["a1x"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1z","text":" & "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a21","text":"32","marks":["a20"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a22","text":"). The High Heaven is reserved for those who believed, led a righteous life, and developed their souls sufficiently."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"a1x","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/11"},{"_key":"a20","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/32"}]}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a26","tone":"muted","style":"attribution","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a28","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a27","text":"United Submitters International / International Community of Submitters / Masjid Tucson"}]}],"reveal":false}]$pt$::jsonb, 'DCw1FX2sidE', 'Appendix 5 — Heaven and Hell'),

-- Appendix 6: Greatness of God
('6', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a4","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“They can never fathom the greatness of God. The whole earth is within His fist on the Day of Resurrection. In fact, the universes are folded within His right hand. Be He glorified; He is much too high above needing any partners.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"39:67"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a8","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"We learn from "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"39:67"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":" that God's greatness is far beyond human comprehension — the verse states that all seven universes are “folded within God's hand.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ai","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":"Supported by the Quran's formidable mathematical code, we are taught that our universe is the smallest and innermost of seven universes ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"41:12"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac","reference":"55:33"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ae","reference":"67:5"},{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":", & "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ag","reference":"72:8-12"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":"). Meanwhile, our scientific advances have shown us that our galaxy, the Milky Way, is 100,000 light years across, and that our universe contains a billion such galaxies and a billion trillion stars, plus countless decillions of heavenly bodies. Our universe is estimated to span distances in excess of 20,000,000,000 light years."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"aj","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ak","text":"Count the Stars"}]}},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"al","tone":"primary","style":"example","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"an","style":"term","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"am","text":"A Thought Experiment"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"at","style":"body","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ao","text":"If we take only a quintillion [1,000,000,000,000,000,000] of the stars and simply count them — one count per second, day and night — this will take 32 billion years (more than the age of the universe). That is how long it would take to just "},{"_type":"span","_key":"ap","text":"count","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"aq","text":" them. God "},{"_type":"span","_key":"ar","text":"created","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"as","text":" them. Such is the greatness of God."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"au","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"av","text":"A Space Odyssey"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"ax","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aw","text":"We can appreciate the vastness of our universe by imagining a journey at the speed of light. Leaving Earth toward the sun, we travel 93,000,000 miles and arrive in 8 minutes. To exit our galaxy would take more than 50,000 years at the speed of light. From the outer limit of the Milky Way, our planet Earth is invisible — not even the most powerful telescope can detect it."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"az","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ay","text":"To reach our nearest neighboring galaxy would require more than 2,000,000 years at the speed of light. To reach the outer limit of our universe: at least 10,000,000,000 years. From that outer limit, even the Milky Way is like a speck of dust in a large room."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a11","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a10","text":"The second universe surrounds our universe. The third universe is larger than the second, and so on. More accurately, our universe should be considered the seventh universe, surrounded by the sixth, which is surrounded by the fifth, and so on. Can you imagine the vastness of the first, outermost universe? No number exists to describe its circumference. This incomprehensible vastness is “within the fist of God's hand.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a13","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a12","text":"From the outer limit of the outermost universe, where is the planet Earth? How significant is it? On this infinitesimal mote, such minuscule creatures as Mary, Jesus, and Muhammad lived. Yet some people set up these powerless humans as gods!"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1b","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a14","text":"God's greatness is represented not only by the fact that He holds the seven universes in His hand, but also by the fact that He fully controls every atom — even subatomic components — everywhere in the greater universe ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a15","reference":"6:59"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a16","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a17","reference":"10:61"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a18","text":", & "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a19","reference":"34:3"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1a","text":")."}]}]$pt$::jsonb, '64v6qoyK3cA', 'Appendix 6 — Greatness of God'),

-- Appendix 7: Why Were We Created?
('7', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a3","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"We are in this world because we committed a horrendous crime, and this life is our chance to redeem ourselves, denounce our crime, and rejoin God's kingdom."}]},"wrapper":"none"}]},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"al","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a7","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4","text":"It all began a few billion years ago when “a feud arose in the Heavenly Society” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a5","reference":"38:69"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a6","text":"). One of the high-ranking creatures, Satan, entertained supercilious thoughts that his God-given powers qualified him to be a god besides God. He thus challenged God's absolute authority. Not only was Satan's idea blasphemous, it was wrong - only God, and no one else, possesses the qualifications and ability to be a god. Consequent to Satan's blasphemy, a division occurred in the Heavenly Society, and all constituents of God's kingdom became classified into four categories:"}]},{"_type":"appendixBadgeList","_key":"ak","ordered":true,"style":"badgeSm","item":"badge","marker":"primary","content":"sm","contentTag":"span","items":[{"_key":"a8","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":"Angels:","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"aa","text":" Creatures who upheld God's absolute authority."}]}},{"_key":"ab","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ac","text":"Animals:","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":" Rebels (animals, trees, stars, etc.) who took advantage of God's offer to repent and re-enter His kingdom. [From Introduction.]"}]}},{"_key":"ae","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":"Jinns:","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"ag","text":" Creatures who agreed with Satan; that he is capable of being a \"god.\""}]}},{"_key":"ah","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ai","text":"Humans:","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"aj","text":" Creatures who did not make up their minds; they failed to make a firm stand with God's absolute authority."}]}}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"am","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"an","text":"The Most Merciful"}]}},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a2d","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"at","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ao","text":"The angels expected God to banish the creatures who did not uphold His absolute authority ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ap","reference":"2:30"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aq","text":"). But God is Most Merciful; He decided to give us a chance to denounce our mistake, and informed the angels that He knew what they did not know ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ar","reference":"2:30"},{"_type":"span","_key":"as","text":"). God knew that some creatures deserved a chance to be redeemed."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"az","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"au","text":"If you claim the ability to fly a plane, the best way to test your claim is to give you a plane and ask you to fly it. This is precisely what God decided to do in response to Satan's claim. God created seven vast universes, then informed the angels that He was appointing Satan as a god on the tiny mote called “Earth” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"av","reference":"2:30"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aw","text":"). The Quranic accounts related to appointing Satan as a temporary “god” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ax","reference":"36:60"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ay","text":") confirm the previous scripture."}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a10","tone":"primary","style":"exampleWide","content":[{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a15","style":"stackXs","tag":"div","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a12","style":"termUpper","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a11","text":"Isaiah 14:13-15"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a14","style":"passage","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a13","text":"You, Lucifer, said in your heart: “I will scale the heavens. Above the stars of God. I will set up my throne. I will take my seat on the Mount of Congregation, in the recesses of the North. I will ascend above the tops of the clouds; I will be like the Most High!”"}]}]},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a1a","style":"stackXs","tag":"div","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a17","style":"termUpper","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a16","text":"Matthew 4:8-10 & Luke 4:5-8"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a19","style":"passage","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a18","text":"The devil then took Jesus up a very high mountain and displayed before him all the kingdoms of the world in their magnificence, promising: “All these will I bestow on you if you prostrate yourself in homage before me.” At this, Jesus said to him, “Away with you, Satan! Scripture has it: ‘You shall worship the Lord your God; Him ALONE shall you adore.’”"}]}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1e","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1b","text":"God's plan called for creating death ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1c","reference":"67:1-2"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1d","text":"), then bringing the humans and jinns into this world. Thus, they start over without any biases, and exercise full freedom to uphold God's absolute authority or Satan's polytheistic theory. To make this crucial decision, every human being receives a message from God advocating His absolute authority, as well as a message from Satan pushing his polytheistic principles."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1i","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1f","text":"To give us a headstart, the Most Merciful gathered all the human beings before Him, prior to sending us to this world, and we bore witness that He alone is our Lord and Master ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1g","reference":"7:172"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1h","text":"). Thus, upholding God's absolute authority is a natural instinct that is an integral part of every human being."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1y","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1j","text":"After putting the rebels to death, the souls of humans and jinns were placed in a special depository. God then created the appropriate bodies to house the souls of jinns and humans during the test period. The first jinn body was made from fire, and Satan was assigned to that body ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1k","reference":"15:27"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l","text":"). The first human body was created from earthly material, clay ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1m","reference":"15:26"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n","text":"), and God assigned the first human soul to that body. The divine plan called for the angels to serve the humans on earth - guard them, drive the wind and rain for them, distribute provisions, etc. This fact is stated in the Quran allegorically: “Your Lord said to the angels, ‘Fall prostrate before Adam.’” Satan of course refused to have anything to do with serving the human race ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1o","reference":"2:34"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1q","reference":"7:11"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1s","reference":"17:61"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1t","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1u","reference":"18:50"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1v","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1w","reference":"20:116"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a20","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1z","text":"While Adam's body remained on earth, the real person, the soul, was admitted into Heaven in the outermost universe. God gave Adam certain commandments, represented by the forbidden tree, and Satan was appointed as Adam's companion to deliver to Adam his satanic message. The rest is history."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a24","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21","text":"Every time a human being is born, a human person is assigned to the new baby from the depository of souls. God assigns the souls in accordance with His knowledge ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a22","reference":"28:68"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a23","text":"). Every soul deserves to be assigned to a certain body, and live under certain circumstances. God alone knows which souls are good and which souls are evil. Our children are assigned to our homes in accordance with God's plan."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a2c","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a25","text":"An independent jinn soul is also assigned to the new human being to represent Satan's point of view. While the physical body of any jinn is reproduced from the parent jinns, the jinn soul is that of an independent individual. Jinns are descendants of Satan ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a26","reference":"7:27"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a27","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a28","reference":"18:50"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a29","text":"). The assigned jinn remains with the human being from birth to death, and serves as the main witness on the Day of Judgment ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2a","reference":"50:23"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2b","text":"). A continuous debate takes place in our heads between the human soul and the jinn soul until both of them are convinced of one point of view."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a2e","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2f","text":"The Original Sin"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"a2h","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2g","text":"Contrary to common belief, the “Original Sin” was not Adam's violation of God's law when he ate from the forbidden tree. The original sin was our failure to uphold God's absolute authority during the Great Feud. If the human person convinces his or her jinn companion to denounce that original sin, and uphold God's absolute authority, both creatures are redeemed to God's eternal kingdom on the Day of Judgment. But if the jinn companion convinces the human being to uphold Satan's idolatrous views, then both creatures are exiled forever from God's kingdom."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a2j","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2i","text":"To promote his point of view, Satan and his representatives advocate the idolization of such powerless creatures as Muhammad, Jesus, Mary, and the saints. Since we are here due to our polytheistic tendencies, most of us are easy prey for Satan."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a2n","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2k","text":"Satan's incompetence as a “god” has already been proven by the prevalence of chaos, disease, accidents, misery, and war throughout his dominion ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2l","reference":"36:66"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2m","text":"). On the other hand, the human beings who denounce Satan, uphold God's absolute authority, and refrain from idolizing powerless and dead creatures like Jesus and Muhammad, are restored to God's protection - they enjoy a perfect life here in this world and forever."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a2x","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2o","text":"Because our life in this world is a series of tests designed to expose our polytheistic ideas, idol worship is the only unforgivable offense ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2p","reference":"4:48"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2q","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2r","reference":"4:116"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2s","text":"). The world is divinely designed to manifest our decision to uphold either God's absolute authority, or Satan's idolatrous views ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2t","reference":"67:1-2"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2u","text":"). The day and the night change constantly to test our willingness to uphold God's laws by observing the Dawn Prayer and fasting during the hottest and longest days. Only those who are totally certain about God's absolute authority are redeemed ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2v","reference":"26:89"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2w","text":")."}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a2z","tone":"muted","style":"attribution","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a31","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a30","text":"United Submitters International / International Community of Submitters / Masjid Tucson"}]}],"reveal":false}]$pt$::jsonb, 'LKrk3v00r80', 'Appendix 7 — Why Were We Created?'),

-- Appendix 8: The Myth of Intercession
('8', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a4","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“Say, ‘All intercession belongs to God.’”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"39:44"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"aa","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"To believe that anyone, other than God, can intercede on our behalf to have our sins forgiven or our wishes fulfilled, is to set up partners with God. This is idolatry. The Quran proclaims that “All intercession belongs to God” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"39:44"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":"), and that there will be “no intercession on the Day of Judgment” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8","reference":"2:254"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ac","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":"The myth of intercession is one of Satan's most effective tricks to dupe millions of people into idol worship. Millions of Christians believe that Jesus will intercede for them at God, and millions of Muslims believe that Muhammad will intercede on their behalf. Consequently, these people idolize Jesus and Muhammad."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"ad","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ae","text":"The Illogical Concept"}]}},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"am","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"ag","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":"The concept of intercession is utterly illogical. Those who believe in Muhammad's intercession, for example, claim that he will ask God to forgive them and admit them into Paradise. They imagine Muhammad on the Day of Judgment choosing the candidates for his intercession. If you ask those who believe in intercession: “How will Muhammad recognize those who deserve his intercession?” they tell you, “God will tell him!”"}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"ah","tone":"primary","style":"example","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"aj","style":"term","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ai","text":"The Logical Absurdity"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"al","style":"body","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ak","text":"According to this concept, a person will go to Muhammad and request his intercession. Muhammad will then ask God whether this person deserves his intercession or not. God will inform Muhammad that the person deserves to go to Paradise. Muhammad will then turn around and tell God that the person deserves to go to Paradise! The blasphemy is obvious; those who believe in intercession make God a secretary of their idol Muhammad. God be glorified."}]}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"an","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ao","text":"What the Quran Actually Says"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"aw","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ap","text":"Since the Quran is the most accurate book, it acknowledges that everyone in Paradise will intercede on behalf of his or her loved ones: “Please God, admit my mother into Paradise.” This intercession will work if the person's mother deserves to go to Paradise ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aq","reference":"2:255"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ar","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"as","reference":"20:109"},{"_type":"span","_key":"at","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"au","reference":"21:28"},{"_type":"span","_key":"av","text":"). Thus, intercession, though it will take place in this manner, is utterly useless."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a26","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":"We learn from the Quran that Abraham, God's beloved servant, could not intercede on behalf of his father ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ay","reference":"9:114"},{"_type":"span","_key":"az","text":"). Noah could not intercede on behalf of his son ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a10","reference":"11:46"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a11","text":"). Muhammad could not intercede on behalf of his uncle ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a12","reference":"111:1-3"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a13","text":") or relatives ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a14","reference":"9:80"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":"). What makes anyone think that a prophet or a saint will intercede on behalf of a perfect stranger?! See "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a16","reference":"2:48"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a18","reference":"2:123"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a19","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1a","reference":"6:51"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1b","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1c","reference":"6:70"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1d","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1e","reference":"6:94"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1f","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1g","reference":"7:53"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1h","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1i","reference":"10:3"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1j","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1k","reference":"19:87"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1m","reference":"26:100"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1o","reference":"30:13"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1q","reference":"32:4"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1s","reference":"36:23"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1t","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1u","reference":"39:44"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1v","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1w","reference":"40:18"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1y","reference":"43:86"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1z","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a20","reference":"53:26"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a21","text":" & "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a22","reference":"74:48"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a23","text":". Muhammad's intercession is in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a24","reference":"25:30"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a25","text":"."}]}]$pt$::jsonb, 'rsZV4eImXzI', 'Appendix 8 — The Myth of Intercession'),

-- Appendix 9: Abraham: Original Messenger of Submission
('9', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a4","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“He has chosen you and has placed no hardship on you in practicing your religion - the religion of your father Abraham. He is the one who named you ‘Submitters’ originally.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"22:78"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ac","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"One of the prevalent myths is that Muhammad was the founder of Islam. Although Islam, total submission to God alone, is the only religion recognized by God since the time of Adam ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"3:19"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8","reference":"3:85"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":"), Abraham is reported in the Quran as the first user of the word “Islam” and the one who called us “Muslims,” i.e., Submitters ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"22:78"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":"). Abraham's exemplary submission to God is demonstrated by his famous willingness to sacrifice his only son, Ismail, when he thought that that was God's command. As it turns out, such a command was in fact from Satan."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"ad","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ae","text":"God Never Ordered Abraham to Sacrifice His Son"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"ao","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":"God is the Most Merciful. He never violates His own law ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ag","reference":"7:28"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":"). Any person who believes that the Most Merciful ordered Abraham to kill his son cannot possibly make it to God's Heaven. Such evil thought about God is grossly blasphemous. Nowhere in the Quran do we see that God ordered Abraham to kill his son. On the contrary, God intervened to save Abraham and Ismail from Satan's plot ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ai","reference":"37:107"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aj","text":"), and He told Abraham: “You believed the dream” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ak","reference":"37:105"},{"_type":"span","_key":"al","text":"). Undoubtedly, it was a dream inspired by Satan. God's irrevocable law is: “God never advocates sin” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"am","reference":"7:28"},{"_type":"span","_key":"an","text":")."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"ap","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aq","text":"Millat Ibrahim"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1c","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ar","text":"Islam is called “Millat Ibrahim” (The Religion of Abraham) throughout the Quran ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"as","reference":"2:130"},{"_type":"span","_key":"at","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"au","reference":"2:135"},{"_type":"span","_key":"av","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aw","reference":"3:95"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ay","reference":"4:125"},{"_type":"span","_key":"az","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a10","reference":"6:161"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a11","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a12","reference":"12:37-38"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a13","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a14","reference":"16:123"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a16","reference":"21:73"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a18","reference":"22:78"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a19","text":"). Moreover, the Quran informs us that Muhammad was a follower of Abraham ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1a","reference":"16:123"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1b","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1u","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1d","text":"Due to a general unawareness of the fact that Abraham was the original messenger of Islam (Submission), many so-called Muslims challenge God: “If the Quran is complete and fully detailed (as claimed by God), where can we find the number of Rak'ahs (units) in each contact prayer (Salat)?” We learn from the Quran that all religious practices of Islam were already established before the Quran's revelation ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1e","reference":"8:35"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1f","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1g","reference":"9:54"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1h","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1i","reference":"16:123"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1j","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1k","reference":"21:73"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1m","reference":"22:27"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1o","reference":"28:27"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p","text":"). Verse "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1q","reference":"16:123"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r","text":" is direct proof that all religious practices in Islam were intact when Muhammad was born. Muhammad was enjoined to “follow the religion of Abraham.” If I ask you to buy a color TV, it is assumed that you know what a color TV is. Similarly, when God enjoined Muhammad to follow the practices of Abraham ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1s","reference":"16:123"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1t","text":"), such practices must have been well known."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a1v","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1w","text":"Divine Preservation of Religious Practices"}]}},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a2u","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1y","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x","text":"Another proof of divine preservation of the Islamic practices given to Abraham is the “Universal Acceptance” of such practices. There is no dispute concerning the number of Rak'ahs in all five daily prayers. This proves the divine preservation of Salat."}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1z","tone":"primary","style":"example","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a21","style":"term","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a20","text":"Mathematical Confirmation of the Five Prayers"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a23","style":"body","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a22","text":"The Quran's mathematical code confirms the number of Rak'ahs in the five prayers 2, 4, 4, 3, and 4, respectively. The number 24434 is a multiple of 19."}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a25","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a24","text":"The Quran deals only with practices that were distorted. For example:"}]},{"_type":"appendixBadgeList","_key":"a2t","ordered":false,"style":"bullet","item":"bullet","marker":"bullet","content":"none","contentTag":"none","bullet":"•","items":[{"_key":"a27","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a28","text":"The distorted ablution is restored in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a29","reference":"5:6"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2a","text":" to its original four steps."}]}},{"_key":"a2c","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2d","text":"The tone of voice during the contact prayers (Salat) was distorted - many Muslims pray silently. This was corrected in the Quran, "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2e","reference":"17:110"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2f","text":"."}]}},{"_key":"a2h","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2i","text":"The fasting during Ramadan was modified in the Quran to allow intercourse during the night ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2j","reference":"2:187"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2k","text":")."}]}},{"_key":"a2m","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2n","text":"Zakat is restored in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2o","reference":"6:141"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2p","text":", and Hajj is restored to the four correct months (see "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2r","text":"Appendix 15","marks":["a2q"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2s","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"a2q","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/15"}]}}]}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a2w","tone":"muted","style":"attribution","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a2y","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2x","text":"United Submitters International / International Community of Submitters / Masjid Tucson"}]}],"reveal":false}]$pt$::jsonb, 'zRNjUb2siNc', 'Appendix 9 — Abraham: Original Messenger of Islam'),

-- Appendix 10: God's Usage of the Plural Tense
('10', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a4","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“I am God. There is no other god besides Me.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"20:14"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1c","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"In the English speaking world, where the trinity doctrine is prevalent, some people are intrigued by God's usage of the plural tense in the Quran. The overwhelming message of the Quran, where there is absolutely no compromise is that “GOD IS ONE” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"2:133"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8","reference":"2:163"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"4:171"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac","reference":"5:73"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ae","reference":"6:19"},{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ag","reference":"9:31"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ai","reference":"12:39"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aj","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ak","reference":"13:16"},{"_type":"span","_key":"al","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"am","reference":"14:48"},{"_type":"span","_key":"an","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ao","reference":"14:52"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ap","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aq","reference":"16:22"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ar","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"as","reference":"16:51"},{"_type":"span","_key":"at","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"au","reference":"18:110"},{"_type":"span","_key":"av","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aw","reference":"21:108"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ay","reference":"22:34"},{"_type":"span","_key":"az","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a10","reference":"37:4"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a11","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a12","reference":"38:65"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a13","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a14","reference":"39:4"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a16","reference":"40:16"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a18","reference":"41:6"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a19","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1a","reference":"112:1"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1b","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1g","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1d","text":"Whenever the first person plural form is used by the Almighty, it invariably indicates participation of other entities, such as the angels. For example, the revelation of this Quran involved participation of the angel Gabriel and the prophet Muhammad. Hence the use of the plural form in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1e","reference":"15:9"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1f","text":": “We revealed this scripture, and we will preserve it.” The plural form here simply reflects the fact that the angel Gabriel and the prophet Muhammad participated in the process of delivering the Quran."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a1h","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1i","text":"Adam vs. Jesus"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1u","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1j","text":"Another example has to do with blowing the breath of life into Adam and Jesus. The creation of Adam took place in heaven and God directly blew into him the breath of life. Thus, the first person singular form is consistently used: "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1k","text":"“I blew into Adam from My spirit”","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l","text":" ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1m","reference":"15:29"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1o","reference":"38:72"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p","text":"). The creation of Jesus, on the other hand, took place on earth, and Gabriel carried God's “word” to Mary. The plural form is consistently used when referring to the creation of Jesus ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1q","reference":"21:91"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1s","reference":"66:12"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1t","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a20","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1v","text":"When God spoke to Moses directly, without the mediation of angels, we see that God is speaking exclusively in the singular tense: "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1w","text":"“I am God. There is no other god besides Me. You shall worship Me alone, and observe the regular contact prayers (Salat) to commemorate Me.”","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x","text":" ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1y","reference":"20:12-14"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1z","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a24","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21","text":"Whenever the worship of God is mentioned, the singular tense is used ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a22","reference":"51:56"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a23","text":")."}]}]$pt$::jsonb, 'ONjW_ayZ1Fo', 'Appendix 10 — God''s Usage of the Plural Tense'),

-- Appendix 11: The Day of Resurrection
('11', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a4","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“The horn is blown, whereupon everyone in the heavens and the earth is struck unconscious, except those spared by God. Then it is blown a second time, whereupon they rise up.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"39:68"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a8","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"All generations of humans and jinns will be resurrected on this earth; about 150 billion of them. But we will not be earthbound. God teaches us through the example of the caterpillar; it turns into a pupa in the cocoon (grave), then exits the cocoon as an airborne butterfly. Similarly, we live here on earth, and when we exit the grave on the Day of Resurrection we will not be earthbound; like the butterfly ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"101:4"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"aq","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":"The earth will shine with the light of God ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"39:69"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":") as He comes to our universe, together with the angels ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac","reference":"89:22"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":"). Since our universe is a temporary dominion for Satan, it cannot stand the physical presence of God ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ae","reference":"7:143"},{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":"). As the Almighty approaches, the stars will crash into one another ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ag","reference":"77:8"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ai","reference":"81:2"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aj","text":"), and the earth will shatter under our feet ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ak","reference":"69:14"},{"_type":"span","_key":"al","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"am","reference":"89:21"},{"_type":"span","_key":"an","text":"). These horrors will not worry the believers ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ao","reference":"21:103"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ap","text":")."}]},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a1l","style":"stackMd","tag":"div","content":[{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"ar","tone":"primary","style":"note","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"at","style":"eyebrowPrimary","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"as","text":"The High Heaven"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ay","style":"bodySm","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"au","text":"Upon arrival of Almighty God, all the humans and jinns will be automatically stratified according to their degree of growth and development. Those who nourished their souls through worshiping God alone, believing in the Hereafter, and leading a righteous life will be strong enough to stay close to God; they will occupy the highest ranks (see "},{"_type":"span","_key":"aw","text":"Appendix 5","marks":["av"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"av","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/5"}]}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"az","tone":"muted","style":"noteMuted","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a11","style":"eyebrowMuted","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a10","text":"The Lower Heaven"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a13","style":"bodySm","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a12","text":"Those who developed their souls to a lesser degree, as well as those who die before the age of forty, will move downward to the Lower Heaven. They will go to the location where they can be as close to God as their degree of growth and development permits them to be."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a14","tone":"muted","style":"noteMuted","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a16","style":"eyebrowMuted","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":"The Purgatory"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1a","style":"bodySm","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":"There will be people who nourished their souls just enough to spare them Hell, but not enough to enter the Lower Heaven. They are neither in Hell, nor in Heaven. They will implore God to admit them into the Lower Heaven ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a18","reference":"7:46-50"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a19","text":"). God will have mercy on them, and will merge the Purgatory into the Lower Heaven."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1b","tone":"destructive","style":"noteDestructive","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1d","style":"eyebrowDestructive","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1c","text":"Hell"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1k","style":"bodySm","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1e","text":"A new, eighth universe will be created to house those who run away from God due to their weakness; they failed to nourish and develop their souls ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1f","reference":"69:17"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1g","text":"). God does not put a single being in Hell; they go to it on their own volition ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1i","text":"Appendix 5","marks":["a1h"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1j","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"a1h","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/5"}]}]}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1n","tone":"muted","style":"attribution","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1p","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1o","text":"United Submitters International / International Community of Submitters / Masjid Tucson"}]}],"reveal":false}]$pt$::jsonb, '1pvtPUiJ4WM', 'Appendix 11 — The Day of Resurrection'),

-- Appendix 12: Role of the Prophet Muhammad
('12', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a4","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“The sole duty of the messenger is to deliver the message.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"5:99"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"au","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"The Prophet's sole mission was to deliver Quran, the whole Quran, and nothing but Quran ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"3:20"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8","reference":"5:48-50"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"5:92"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac","reference":"5:99"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ae","reference":"6:19"},{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ag","reference":"13:40"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ai","reference":"16:35"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aj","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ak","reference":"16:82"},{"_type":"span","_key":"al","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"am","reference":"24:54"},{"_type":"span","_key":"an","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ao","reference":"29:18"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ap","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aq","reference":"42:48"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ar","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"as","reference":"64:12"},{"_type":"span","_key":"at","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a16","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"av","text":"Delivering the Quran was such a momentous and noble mission that the Prophet did not have any time to do anything else. Moreover, the Prophet was enjoined in the strongest words from issuing any religious teachings besides the Quran ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aw","reference":"69:38-47"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":"). He was even enjoined from explaining the Quran ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ay","reference":"75:15-19"},{"_type":"span","_key":"az","text":") — God is the only teacher of the Quran ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a10","reference":"55:1-2"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a11","text":") and the Quran is the best Hadith ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a12","reference":"39:23"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a13","text":" & "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a14","reference":"45:6"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":")."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a17","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a18","text":"Hadith & Sunna: A Later Fabrication"}]}},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a1p","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1e","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a19","text":"These Quranic facts are manifested in the historical reality that the words and actions (Hadith & Sunna) attributed to the Prophet did not appear until the second century after his death. The Quran has prophesied the fabrication of Hadith and Sunna by the Prophet's enemies ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1a","reference":"6:112-115"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1b","text":"). The Quran teaches us that it was God's will to permit the invention of Hadith and Sunna to serve as criteria for exposing those who believe only with their lips, not in their hearts. Those who are attracted to Hadith and Sunna are proven to be false believers ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1c","reference":"6:113"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1d","text":"). Ironically, the books of Hadith report the Prophet's orders to write down nothing from him except the Quran! Shown below are two such Hadiths taken from the Hadithists' most reliable sources, Sahih Muslim and Is-haah Ahmad Ibn Hanbal:"}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1f","tone":"primary","style":"blockquote","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1h","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1g","text":"The Prophet said, “Do not write down anything from me except the Quran.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1j","style":"notePlain","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1i","text":"[Ahmed, Vol. 1, Page 171, and Sahih Muslim]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1k","tone":"primary","style":"blockquote","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1m","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l","text":"This Hadith states that the Prophet maintained his anti-Hadith stand until death."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1o","style":"notePlain","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n","text":"[Ahmed, Vol. 1, Page 192]"}]}],"reveal":false}]}]$pt$::jsonb, 'vyhPUP1Letc', 'Appendix 12 — Role of the Prophet Muhammad'),

-- Appendix 13: The First Pillar of Submission
('13', $pt$[{"_type":"block","_key":"a4","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1","text":"Verse "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2","reference":"3:18"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3","text":" states the First Pillar of Islam (Submission):"}]},{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a8","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a5","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6","text":"“God bears witness that there is no other god besides Him, and so do the angels and those who possess knowledge.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a7","reference":"3:18"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ac","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":"This most crucial pillar has been distorted. Millions of Muslims have adopted Satan’s polytheistic version, and insist upon mentioning the name of Muhammad besides the name of God. However, the Quran’s great criterion in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"39:45"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":" stamps such Muslims as disbelievers:"}]},{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"ag","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"ad","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ae","text":"“When God ALONE is mentioned, the hearts of those who do disbelieve in the Hereafter shrink with aversion, but when others are mentioned with Him, they become satisfied.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"af","reference":"39:45"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1d","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":"I have conducted extensive research into this criterion, and I have reached a startling conclusion: the idol worshipers who do not uphold the First Pillar of Islam as dictated in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ai","reference":"3:18"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aj","text":" are forbidden by God from uttering the correct "},{"_type":"span","_key":"ak","text":"Shahadah","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"al","text":". They simply cannot say: “"},{"_type":"span","_key":"am","text":"Ash-hadu Allaa Elaaha Ellaa Allah","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"an","text":"” by itself, without mentioning the name of Muhammad. Try it with any idol worshiper who claims to be a Muslim. Challenge them to say: “"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ao","text":"Ash-hadu Allaa Elaaha Ellaa Allah","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"ap","text":".” They can never say it. Since this is the religion of Abraham ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aq","reference":"2:130"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ar","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"as","reference":"2:135"},{"_type":"span","_key":"at","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"au","reference":"3:95"},{"_type":"span","_key":"av","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aw","reference":"4:125"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ay","reference":"6:161"},{"_type":"span","_key":"az","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a10","reference":"12:37"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a11","text":"-"},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a12","reference":"12:38"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a13","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a14","reference":"16:123"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a16","reference":"22:78"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":"; "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a19","text":"Appendix 9","marks":["a18"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1a","text":"), the ONLY creed must be “"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1b","text":"LAA ELAAHA ELLAA ALLAH","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1c","text":" (there is no god except the One God)”. Muhammad did not exist on earth before Abraham."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"a18","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/9"}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a1e","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1f","text":"A Gross Blasphemy"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1r","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1g","text":"There is no greater blasphemy than distorting the Quran to idolize the prophet Muhammad against his will. Verse 19 of Sura “Muhammad” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1h","reference":"47:19"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1i","text":") states: “You shall know that there is no god except the one God.” Shown below is a photocopy of the regular logo of a Muslim publication "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1j","text":"THE REVIEW OF RELIGIONS","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1k","text":" (The London Mosque, 16 Gressenhall Road, London SW18 5QL, England). Using the Quran’s calligraphic style, the publishers of "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l","text":"THE REVIEW OF RELIGIONS","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1m","text":" added the phrase “"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n","text":"Muhammad Rasool Allah","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1o","text":"” in such a way that gives a false impression that such is the Quranic statement of "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1p","reference":"47:19"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1q","text":". What a blasphemy!"}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1s","tone":"primary","style":"centered","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1u","style":"base","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1t","text":"You shall know that there is no god besides the One God, Allah. Muhammad is a messenger of God."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1w","style":"eyebrowMuted","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1v","text":"[ The blasphemy ]"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1y","style":"noteItalic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x","text":"Typical Example of the Distorted Islam"}]}]}]$pt$::jsonb, 'Ia54Hfyclv8', 'Appendix 13 — The First Pillar of Islam'),

-- Appendix 14: Predestination
('14', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a4","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“Anything that happens on earth, or to you, has already been recorded, even before the creation. This is easy for God to do.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"57:22"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ak","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"We are absolutely free to believe or disbelieve in God. It is God's will that we will ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"18:29"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8","reference":"25:57"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"73:19"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac","reference":"74:37"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ae","reference":"76:29"},{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ag","reference":"78:39"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ai","reference":"80:12"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aj","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"au","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"al","text":"After committing our original sin ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"an","text":"Appendix 7","marks":["am"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"ao","text":"), God gave us a chance to denounce our crime and accept His absolute authority ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ap","reference":"33:72"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aq","text":"). But we decided that we wanted to see a demonstration of Satan's competence as a god. Many people protest the fact that God has created them, to put them through this gruesome test. Obviously, such people are not aware that [1] they have committed a horrendous crime (Introduction & "},{"_type":"span","_key":"as","text":"Appendix 7","marks":["ar"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"at","text":"), and [2] that they were given a chance to denounce their crime and redeem themselves, but they chose to go through the test."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"am","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/7"},{"_key":"ar","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/7"}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"av","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aw","text":"Free Will Within God's Full Knowledge"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"a10","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":"We learn from "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ay","reference":"57:22"},{"_type":"span","_key":"az","text":" that our lives, along with everything else around us, are pre-recorded on something like a videotape. God fully knows what kind of decision each of us is destined to make; He knows which of us are going to Heaven and which are going to Hell. Even before we were born into this world, God knew which souls are good and which souls are evil. As far as God's omniscience is concerned, we can imagine a stamp on everyone's forehead that says “Heaven” or “Hell.” Yet, as far as we are concerned, we are totally free to side with God's absolute authority, or Satan's polytheistic views. Predestination, therefore, is a fact as far as God is concerned, not as far as we are concerned."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a11","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a12","text":"Quranic Examples"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1c","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a13","text":"This understanding explains the numerous verses stating that “God guides whomever He wills, and misleads whomever He wills.” Based on His knowledge, God assigns our souls to the circumstances that we deserve. When God said to the angels, “I know what you do not know” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a14","reference":"2:30"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":"), this meant that some of us deserved a chance to redeem ourselves. One example of God's guidance for those who deserve guidance is found in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a16","reference":"21:51"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":": “We granted Abraham his guidance, for we were fully aware of him.” In other words, God knew that Abraham was a good soul who deserved to be guided, and God granted him his guidance and understanding. Another good example is stated in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a18","reference":"12:24"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a19","text":". Joseph fell for the Egyptian nobleman's wife, and almost committed adultery “if it were not that he saw a sign from his Lord.” God teaches us in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1a","reference":"12:24"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1b","text":" that He “diverted evil and sin from Joseph, for he was one of My devoted worshipers.” Was it Joseph who controlled his lust? Or, was it God's protection from sin that rendered him chaste? Such is predestination."}]}]$pt$::jsonb, 'ycRUmiOuRiM', 'Appendix 14 — Predestination'),

-- Appendix 15: Religious Duties: Gift from God
('15', $pt$[{"_type":"block","_key":"af","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1","text":"When Abraham implored God in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2","reference":"14:40"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3","text":", he did not ask for wealth or health; the gift he implored for was: "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a4","text":"“Please God, make me one who observes the contact prayers (Salat).”","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":" The religious duties instituted by God are in fact a great gift from Him. They constitute the nourishment required for the growth and development of our souls. Without such nourishment, we cannot survive the immense energy associated with God’s physical presence on the Day of Judgment. Belief in God does not by itself guarantee our redemption; we must also nourish our souls ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"6:158"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8","reference":"10:90"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":"–"},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"10:92"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":"). Additionally, "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac","reference":"15:99"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":" states that observing the religious duties instituted by God is our means of attaining certainty: "},{"_type":"span","_key":"ae","text":"“Worship your Lord in order to attain certainty.”","marks":["em"]}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"ag","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":"The Contact Prayers (Salat)"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"block","_key":"aj","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ai","text":"The five daily contact prayers are the main meals for the soul. While a soul may attain some growth and development by leading a righteous life, and without observing the contact prayers, this would be like surviving on snacks without regular meals."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"an","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ak","text":"We learn from "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"al","reference":"2:37"},{"_type":"span","_key":"am","text":" that we can establish contact with God by uttering the specific Arabic words given to us by God. Sura 1, The Key, is a mathematically composed combination of sounds that unlocks the door between us and God:"}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a1i","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ao","text":"The Five Daily Prayers"}]},"table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ap","text":"Prayer"}]},"headerStyle":"left","cellStyle":"mediumSm"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aq","text":"Time"}]},"headerStyle":"left","cellStyle":"faded"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ar","text":"Units"}]},"headerStyle":"leftMono","cellStyle":"monoPrimaryBold"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"as","text":"Reference"}]},"headerStyle":"left","cellStyle":"muted"}],"rows":[{"_key":"at","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"au","text":"Dawn"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"av","text":"During the two hours before sunrise"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aw","text":"2"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":"11:114, 24:58"}]}]},{"_key":"ay","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"az","text":"Noon"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a10","text":"When the sun declines from its highest point at noon"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a11","text":"4"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a12","text":"17:78"}]}]},{"_key":"a13","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a14","text":"Afternoon"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":"During the 3-4 hours preceding sunset"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a16","text":"4"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":"2:238"}]}]},{"_key":"a18","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a19","text":"Sunset"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1a","text":"After sunset"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1b","text":"3"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1c","text":"11:114"}]}]},{"_key":"a1d","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1e","text":"Night"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1f","text":"After the twilight disappears from the sky"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1g","text":"4"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1h","text":"24:58"}]}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1m","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1j","text":"The Friday noon congregational prayer is an obligatory duty upon every Submitting man and woman ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1k","reference":"62:9"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l","text":"). Failure to observe the Friday Prayer is a gross offense."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1o","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n","text":"Each contact prayer is valid if observed anytime during the period it becomes due until the next prayer becomes due. Once missed, a given contact prayer is a missed opportunity that cannot be made up; one can only repent and ask forgiveness. The five prayers consist of 2, 4, 4, 3, and 4 units (Rak’ahs), respectively."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a26","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p","text":"The proof that Salat was already established through Abraham is found in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1q","reference":"8:35"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1s","reference":"9:54"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1t","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1u","reference":"16:123"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1v","text":", & "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1w","reference":"21:73"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1x","text":". This most important duty in Islam (Submission) has been so severely distorted that the contact prayers (Salat) have become a practice in idolatry for the vast majority of Muslims. Although the Quran commands that our contact prayers must be devoted to God alone ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1y","reference":"20:14"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1z","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a20","reference":"39:3"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a21","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a22","reference":"39:45"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a23","text":"), today’s Muslims insist on commemorating “Muhammad and his family” and “Abraham and his family” during their prayers. This renders the prayers null and void ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a24","reference":"39:65"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a25","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a28","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a27","text":"The following text, pertaining to the miracles confirming the contact prayers, is excerpted (with minor editing), from the January 1990 issues of the Submitter’s Perspective (the regular and special bonus issues), as written by Dr. Rashad Khalifa:"}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a29","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2a","text":"Awesome Mathematical Miracle Confirms All The 5 Contact Prayers"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a2l","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a2d","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2b","text":"[1]","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2c","text":" Sura 1 is God’s gift to us, to establish contact with Him (Salat). Write the sura number and the number of verses next to each other and you get 17, the total number of units in the 5 daily prayers."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a2g","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2e","text":"[2]","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2f","text":" Let us write down the sura number, followed by the number of every verse in the sura. This is what we get:"}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a2h","style":"sequence","text":"sequence","value":"1 1 2 3 4 5 6 7"},{"_type":"block","_key":"a2k","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2j","text":"This number is a multiple of 19."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a3i","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2m","text":"Properties of Sura 1, The Key"}]},"table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2n","text":"Verse No"}]},"headerStyle":"leftMono","cellStyle":"monoPrimarySemibold"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2o","text":"No of Letters"}]},"headerStyle":"rightMono","cellStyle":"monoRightAlt"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2p","text":"Gematrical Value"}]},"headerStyle":"rightMono","cellStyle":"monoRightAlt"}],"rows":[{"_key":"a2q","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2r","text":"1"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2s","text":"19"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2t","text":"786"}]}]},{"_key":"a2u","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2v","text":"2"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2w","text":"17"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2x","text":"581"}]}]},{"_key":"a2y","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2z","text":"3"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a30","text":"12"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a31","text":"618"}]}]},{"_key":"a32","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a33","text":"4"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a34","text":"11"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a35","text":"241"}]}]},{"_key":"a36","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a37","text":"5"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a38","text":"19"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a39","text":"836"}]}]},{"_key":"a3a","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3b","text":"6"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3c","text":"18"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3d","text":"1072"}]}]},{"_key":"a3e","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3f","text":"7"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3g","text":"43"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3h","text":"6009"}]}]}]},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a4g","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a3l","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3j","text":"[3]","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3k","text":" Now, let us replace each verse number by the number of letters in that verse. This is what we get:"}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a3m","style":"sequence","text":"sequence","value":"1 19 17 12 11 19 18 43"},{"_type":"block","_key":"a3p","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3o","text":"also a multiple of 19. Theoretically, one can alter the letters of Sura 1, and still keep the same number of letters, however, the following mathematical phenomena rule out that possibility. For the gematrical value of every single letter is taken into consideration. Here it is:"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a3s","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3q","text":"[4]","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3r","text":" Let us include the gematrical value of every verse, and write it down following the number of letters in each verse:"}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a3t","style":"sequence","text":"sequence","value":"1 19 786 17 581 12 618 11 241 19 836 18 1072 43 6009"},{"_type":"block","_key":"a3w","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3v","text":"also a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a3z","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3x","text":"[5]","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3y","text":" Now, let us add the number of each verse, to be followed by the number of letters in that verse, then the gematrical value of that verse. This is what we get:"}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a40","style":"sequence","text":"sequence","value":"1 1 19 786 2 17 581 3 12 618 4 11 241 5 19 836 6 18 1072 7 43 6009"},{"_type":"block","_key":"a43","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a42","text":"a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a46","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a44","text":"[6]","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a45","text":" Instead of the gematrical values of every verse, let us write down the gematrical values of every individual letter in Sura 1. This truly awesome miracle, shows that the resulting long number, consisting of 274 digits, is also a multiple of 19. ALLAHU AKBAR."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a47","style":"sequence","text":"sequence","value":"1 7 1 19 2 60 40 1 30 30 5 1 30 200 8 40 50 1 30 200 8 10 40 2 17 ... 50"},{"_type":"block","_key":"a4a","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a49","text":"This number starts with the sura number, followed by the number of verses in the sura, followed by the verse number, followed by the number of letters in this verse, followed by the gematrical values of every letter in this verse, followed by the number of the next verse, followed by the number of letters in this verse, followed by the gematrical values of every letter in this verse, and so on to the end of the sura. Thus, the last component is 50, the value of “N” (last letter)."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a4d","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4b","text":"[7]","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a4c","text":" Since I cannot write very long numbers here, let us substitute [*] for the long number consisting of the number of every verse, followed by the number of letters in the verse, followed by the gematrical value of every individual letter in the verse. If we write down the number of the sura, followed by its number of verses, we get 17, the number of units (Rak’aas) in the 5 daily prayers. Next to the 17, write down the number of the first prayer (1), followed by its number of Rak’aas, which is 2, then two [*]’s, followed by the number of the second prayer (2), followed by the number of Rak’aas in the second prayer (4), followed by four [*]’s, and so on. Not only is the resulting long number a multiple of 19, but also the number of its component digits is 4636 (19x244) ...."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a4e","style":"sequence","text":"sequence","value":"17 12[*][*] 24[*][*][*][*] 34[*][*][*][*] 43[*][*][*] 54[*][*][*][*]"}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a4h","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4i","text":"Confirmation of Friday Prayers"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a4s","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a4l","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4j","text":"[8]","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a4k","text":" Since the Friday prayer consists of two sermons and two Rak’aas (total is still 4 units), we read only 15 “Keys” on Friday, compared with 17 on the other days. Abdullah Arik discovered that if we replace the 17 by 15 in the long number in [7], we must remove two “Keys” from the noon prayer to get a multiple of 19. This confirms the Friday Prayer, at noon, with 2 “Keys.” The long number shown below represents Friday’s five prayers; it is a multiple of 19."}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a4m","style":"sequence","text":"sequence","value":"15 12[*][*] 24[*][*] 34[*][*][*][*] 43[*][*][*] 54[*][*][*][*]"},{"_type":"block","_key":"a4p","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4o","text":"Please note that, Dr. Khalifa indicated, there are other similar patterns that result in multiples of 19. For example, a short representation of “The Key” consists of the Sura number (1), followed by the number of verses (7), followed by the total number of letters in Sura 1 (139), followed by the total gematrical value of the whole sura (10143). The resulting number (1713910143) representing [*] can be used in the above patterns, without including the sequence number of the prayers. The resultant numbers for both regular and Friday prayer representations are multiples of 19. For example, here is the pattern for the regular prayers:"}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a4q","style":"sequence","text":"sequence","value":"17 2[*][*] 4[*][*][*][*] 4[*][*][*][*] 3[*][*][*] 4[*][*][*][*]"}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a4t","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4u","text":"“The Key” (Al-Fateha) Must Be Recited in Arabic"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"block","_key":"a4x","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4v","text":"[9]","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a4w","text":" The first sura in the Quran is mathematically composed in a manner that challenges and stumps the greatest mathematicians on earth. Now we appreciate the fact that when we recite Sura 1, “The Key,” during our Contact Prayers, something happens in the universe, and we establish contact with our Creator. The result is perfect happiness, now and forever. By contacting our Almighty Creator 5 times a day, we nourish and develop our souls in preparation for the Big Day when we meet God. Only those who nourish their soul will be able to withstand and enjoy the physical presence of Almighty God."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a51","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a4y","text":"All submitters, of all nationalities, recite the words of “The Key” which were written by God Himself, and given to us to establish contact with Him ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a4z","reference":"2:37"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a50","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a53","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a52","text":"Edip Yuksel’s discovery adds to the awesomeness of “The Key” and proclaims clearly that it must be recited in Arabic."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a55","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a54","text":"When you recite “The Key” in Arabic, your lips touch each other precisely 19 times."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a57","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a56","text":"Furthermore, your lips touch each other where the letters “B” and “M” occur. There are 4 “B’s” and 15 “M’s” and this adds up to 19. The gematrical value of the 4 “B’s” is 4x2=8, and the gematrical value of the 15 “M’s” is 15x40=600. The total gematrical value of the 4 “B’s” and 15 “M’s” is 608, that is 19x32."}]},{"_type":"appendixDataTable","_key":"a83","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a58","text":"Lip-Touch Letters in Al-Fateha"}]},"table":"plain","columns":[{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a59","text":"#"}]},"headerStyle":"leftMono","cellStyle":"monoMutedAlt"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5a","text":"Word"}]},"headerStyle":"left","cellStyle":"monoAlt"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5b","text":"Letter"}]},"headerStyle":"left","cellStyle":"primary"},{"header":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5c","text":"Value"}]},"headerStyle":"rightMono","cellStyle":"monoRightAlt"}],"rows":[{"_key":"a5d","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5e","text":"1."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5f","text":"Bism"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5g","text":"B"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5h","text":"2"}]}]},{"_key":"a5i","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5j","text":"2."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5k","text":"Bism"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5l","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5m","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a5n","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5o","text":"3."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5p","text":"Rahman"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5q","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5r","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a5s","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5t","text":"4."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5u","text":"Rahim"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5v","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5w","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a5x","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5y","text":"5."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5z","text":"Al-Hamdu"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a60","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a61","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a62","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a63","text":"6."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a64","text":"Rub"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a65","text":"B"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a66","text":"2"}]}]},{"_key":"a67","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a68","text":"7."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a69","text":"`Alamin"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6a","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6b","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a6c","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6d","text":"8."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6e","text":"Rahman"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6f","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6g","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a6h","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6i","text":"9."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6j","text":"Rahim"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6k","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6l","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a6m","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6n","text":"10."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6o","text":"Malik"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6p","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6q","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a6r","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6s","text":"11."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6t","text":"Yawm"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6u","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6v","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a6w","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6x","text":"12."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6y","text":"Na'budu"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a6z","text":"B"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a70","text":"2"}]}]},{"_key":"a71","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a72","text":"13."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a73","text":"Mustaqim"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a74","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a75","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a76","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a77","text":"14."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a78","text":"Mustaqim"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a79","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7a","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a7b","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7c","text":"15."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7d","text":"An`amta"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7e","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7f","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a7g","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7h","text":"16."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7i","text":"`Alayhim"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7j","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7k","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a7l","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7m","text":"17."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7n","text":"Maghdub"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7o","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7p","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a7q","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7r","text":"18."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7s","text":"Maghdub"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7t","text":"B"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7u","text":"2"}]}]},{"_key":"a7v","style":"data","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7w","text":"19."}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7x","text":"`Alayhim"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7y","text":"M"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a7z","text":"40"}]}]},{"_key":"a80","style":"totalsBand","cells":[{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a81","text":"Total"}]},{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a82","text":"608 (19x32)"}]}],"cellStyles":["semibold","monoPrimaryBoldRight"]}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a84","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a85","text":"Confirmation of the 5 Daily Prayers, Number of Bowings (Ruku'), Prostrations (Sujood), and Tashahhud"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a8n","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a8i","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a86","text":"[10]","marks":["strong"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a87","text":" One of the common challenges... is: “If the Quran is complete and detailed (as claimed in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a88","reference":"6:19"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a89","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8a","reference":"6:38"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8b","text":", & "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8c","reference":"6:114"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8d","text":"), where are the details of the Contact Prayers (Salat)?” These people ask this question because they are not aware that the Quran informs us that the Contact Prayers came from Abraham ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8e","reference":"21:73"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8f","text":" & "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8g","reference":"22:78"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8h","text":").... Saeed Talari... wrote down the numbers of the prayers with their bowings, prostrations and Tashahhuds...:"}]},{"_type":"appendixCode","_key":"a8j","style":"sequence","text":"sequence","value":"1 1 2 2 4 1 2 4 4 8 2 3 4 4 8 2 4 3 3 6 2 5 4 4 8 2"},{"_type":"block","_key":"a8m","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a8l","text":"This long number consists of the sura that we recite in the 5 prayers (1) followed by the number of the first prayer (1), then the number of “Keys” that we recite in this prayer (2), then the number of bowings (Ruku`) (2), then the number of prostrations (4), then the number of Tashahhuds (in the sitting position) (1), then the number of the second prayer (2), then the number of “Keys” that we recite in the second prayer (4), then the number of bowings (Ruku`) in this prayer (4), then the number of prostrations (8), then the number of Tashahhuds (2), then the number of the third prayer (3), and so on to the last prayer. This long number is a multiple of 19, and this confirms the minutest details of the prayers, even in the numbers of Ruku`, Sujud, and Tashahhud."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a8o","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a8p","text":"The Obligatory Charity (Zakat)"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"block","_key":"a8z","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a8q","text":"Zakat must be given away “on the day of harvest” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8r","reference":"6:141"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8s","text":"). Whenever we receive “net income,” we must set aside 2.5% and give it to the specified recipients — the parents, relatives, orphans, the poor, and the traveling alien, in this order ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8t","reference":"2:215"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8u","text":"). The vital importance of Zakat is reflected in God’s law: "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8v","text":"“My mercy encompasses all things, but I will specify it for the righteous who give Zakat”","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8w","text":" ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8x","reference":"7:156"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a8y","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a91","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a90","text":"Zakat must be carefully calculated and given away on a regular basis whenever we receive any income. Government taxes should be deducted, but not other expenses such as debts, mortgages, and living expenses. If one does not know needy persons, he or she may give the Zakat to a mosque or charitable organization with the distinct purpose of helping poor people. Charities given to mosques or hospitals or organizations cannot be considered Zakat."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a92","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a93","text":"Fasting"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"block","_key":"a99","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a94","text":"The full details of fasting are given in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a95","reference":"2:183"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a96","text":"–"},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a97","reference":"2:187"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a98","text":"."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a9a","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9b","text":"Pilgrimage: Hajj & 'Umrah"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"block","_key":"a9o","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9c","text":"Once in a lifetime, Hajj and ‘Umrah are decreed for those who can afford it. Pilgrimage commemorates Abraham’s exemplary submission to God ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9e","text":"Appendix 9","marks":["a9d"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9f","text":"), and must be observed during the four Sacred Months — Zul-Hijjah, Muharram, Safar, & Rabi` I (12th, 1st, 2nd, 3rd months) ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a9g","reference":"2:197"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9h","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a9i","reference":"9:2"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9j","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a9k","reference":"9:36"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9l","text":"). ‘Umrah can be observed any time. Like all other duties in Islam, Hajj has been distorted. Most Muslims observe Hajj only during a few days in Zul-Hijjah, and they consider Rajab, Zul-Qi’dah, Zul-Hijjah, and Muharram (7th, 11th, 12th, 1st months) to be the Sacred Months. This is a distortion that is strongly condemned ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a9m","reference":"9:37"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9n","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"a9d","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/9"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"aa2","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9p","text":"The pilgrimage begins with a bath or shower, followed by a state of sanctity called “Ihraam,” where the male pilgrim wears seamless sheets of material, and the woman wears a modest dress ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a9q","reference":"2:196"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9r","text":"). Throughout Hajj, the pilgrim abstains from sexual intercourse, vanities such as shaving and cutting the hair, arguments, misconduct, and bad language ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a9s","reference":"2:197"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9t","text":"). Cleanliness, bathing, and regular hygiene practices are encouraged. Upon arrival at the Sacred Mosque in Mecca, the pilgrim walks around the Ka`bah seven times, while glorifying and praising God ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a9u","reference":"2:125"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9v","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a9w","reference":"22:26"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9x","text":"–"},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a9y","reference":"22:29"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9z","text":"). The common formula is: “Labbayka Allaahumma Labbayk” (My God, I have responded to You). “Labbayka Laa Shareeka Laka Labbayk” (I have responded to You, and I proclaim that there is no other god besides You; I have responded to You). The next step is to walk the half-mile distance between the knolls of Safa and Marwah seven times, with occasional trotting ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa0","reference":"2:158"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aa1","text":"). This completes the ‘Umrah portion of the pilgrimage."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"aaf","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aa3","text":"The pilgrim then goes to ‘Arafat to spend a day of worship, meditation, and glorification of God, from dawn to sunset ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa4","reference":"2:198"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aa5","text":"). After sunset, the pilgrim goes to Muzdalifah where the Night Prayer is observed, and 21 pebbles are picked up for the symbolic stoning of Satan at Mina. From Muzdalifah, the pilgrim goes to Mina to spend two or three days ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa6","reference":"2:203"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aa7","text":"). On the first morning at Mina, the pilgrim offers an animal sacrifice to feed the poor and to commemorate God’s intervention to save Ismail and Abraham from Satan’s trick ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa8","reference":"37:107"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aa9","text":", "},{"_type":"span","_key":"aab","text":"Appendix 9","marks":["aaa"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"aac","text":"). The stoning ceremonies symbolize rejection of Satan’s polytheism and are done by throwing seven pebbles at each of three stations, while glorifying God ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aad","reference":"15:34"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aae","text":"). The pilgrim then returns to Mecca and observes a farewell circumvolution of the Ka`bah seven times."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"aaa","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/9"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"aah","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aag","text":"Unfortunately, most of today’s Muslim pilgrims make it a custom to visit the prophet Muhammad’s tomb where they commit the most flagrant acts of idolatry and thus nullify their Hajj. The Quran consistently talks about “The Sacred Mosque,” while today’s Muslims talk about “The Two Sacred Mosques!” In a glaring act of idolatry, the Muslims have set up Muhammad’s tomb as another “Sacred Mosque!” This is a blasphemous violation of the Quran, and, ironically, even violates Hadith. The Hadith shown below illustrates this strange irony:"}]},{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"aal","style":"tight","body":"base","refStyle":"sans","entries":[{"_key":"aai","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aaj","text":"“God has cursed the Jews and Christians for turning the tombs of their prophets into mosques.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aak","text":"Translation of this false statement. [Bukhari, Nawawi Edition, Vol. 6, Page 14]"}]}}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"aam","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aan","text":"Physical Benefits"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"block","_key":"aap","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aao","text":"In addition to their invaluable spiritual benefits, there is a plethora of physical, economic, and health benefits from observing the contact prayers (Salat), obligatory charity (Zakat), fasting during the month of Ramadan, and Hajj."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"aar","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aaq","text":"Observing the Dawn prayer interrupts long periods of stillness during sleep; this is now proven to help prevent arthritis. Also, getting up early in the morning helps combat depression and other psychological problems. The prostration position which is repeated during the contact prayers expands the blood vessels in our brains to accommodate more blood, and this prevents headaches. The repeated bending of the back and the joints is a healthful exercise. All these are scientifically established facts."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"aat","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aas","text":"The ablutions required prior to the contact prayers encourage us to use the toilet more frequently. This habit protects us from a common and devastating cancer, colon cancer. Harmful chemicals are excreted in the urine and fecal matter. If these excretions are kept in the colon for prolonged periods of time, the harmful materials are re-absorbed into the body, and cause cancer."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"aav","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aau","text":"Fasting during the month of Ramadan restores our expanded stomachs to their normal sizes, lowers our blood pressure through temporary dehydration, rids the body of harmful toxins, gives our kidneys a much needed rest, and reduces our weight by removing excessive and harmful fat."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"aax","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aaw","text":"Zakat charity and Hajj pilgrimage have far reaching economic and social benefits."}]}]$pt$::jsonb, NULL, NULL),

-- Appendix 16: Dietary Prohibition
('16', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a4","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“Say, ‘I do not find in what was revealed to me anything prohibited for any eater unless it is (1) carrion, (2) running blood, (3) the meat of pigs, for it is unclean, and (4) meat blasphemously dedicated to other than God.’ If one is forced to eat these without being malicious or deliberate, then your Lord is Forgiver, Most Merciful.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"6:145"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ae","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"The Quran teaches that God is extremely displeased with those who prohibit anything that was not specifically prohibited in the Quran ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"16:112"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":"–"},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8","reference":"16:116"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":"). The upholding of any prohibitions not specifically mentioned in the Quran is tantamount to idolatry ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"6:142"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":"–"},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac","reference":"6:152"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":"). Such prohibitions represent some other god(s) besides God. If you worship God ALONE, you will uphold His teachings ALONE and honor the commandments and prohibitions instituted only by Him."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"au","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":"The absolute specificity of dietary prohibitions in the Quran is best illustrated in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ag","reference":"6:145"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":"–"},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ai","reference":"6:146"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aj","text":". We learn from these two verses that when God prohibits “meat,” He prohibits “meat” and nothing else, and when He prohibits “fat,” that is what He specifically prohibits. These two verses inform us that “the meat” of pigs is prohibited, not “the fat.” Obviously, God knew that in many countries, lard would be used in baked goods and other food products, and that such usage does not render the foods Haraam (prohibited). The Quran specifically prohibits four meats ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ak","reference":"2:173"},{"_type":"span","_key":"al","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"am","reference":"5:3"},{"_type":"span","_key":"an","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ao","reference":"6:142"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ap","text":"–"},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aq","reference":"6:145"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ar","text":", and "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"as","reference":"16:112"},{"_type":"span","_key":"at","text":"):"}]},{"_type":"appendixListCard","_key":"a16","caption":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"av","text":"The Four Prohibited Meats ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aw","reference":"6:145"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":")"}]},"item":"rowWide","marker":"primaryCompactSpaced","content":"bodySm","contentTag":"span","items":[{"_key":"ay","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"az","text":"carrion"}]}},{"_key":"a10","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a11","text":"running blood"}]}},{"_key":"a12","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a13","text":"the meat of pigs, for it is unclean"}]}},{"_key":"a14","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":"meat blasphemously dedicated to other than God"}]}}]}]$pt$::jsonb, 'o7GJwyv_ZRM', 'Appendix 16 — Dietary Prohibitions'),

-- Appendix 17: Death
('17', $pt$[{"_type":"block","_key":"am","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1","text":"Death is a great mystery to most people. Not so for the students of the Quran. We learn that death is exactly like sleeping; complete with dreams ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2","reference":"6:60"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a4","reference":"40:46"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"). The period between death and resurrection passes like one night of sleep ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"2:259"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8","reference":"6:60"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"10:45"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac","reference":"16:21"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ae","reference":"18:11"},{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ag","reference":"18:19"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ai","reference":"18:25"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aj","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ak","reference":"30:55"},{"_type":"span","_key":"al","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"au","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"an","text":"At the moment of death, everyone knows his or her destiny; Heaven or Hell. For the disbelievers, death is a horrible event; the angels beat them on the faces and rear ends as they snatch away their souls ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ao","reference":"8:50"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ap","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aq","reference":"47:27"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ar","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"as","reference":"79:1"},{"_type":"span","_key":"at","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a15","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"av","text":"Consistently, the Quran talks about two deaths, the first death took place when we failed to make a stand with God’s absolute authority ("},{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":"Appendix 7","marks":["aw"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"ay","text":"). That first death lasted until we were born into this world. The second death terminates our life in this world ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"az","reference":"2:28"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a10","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a11","reference":"22:66"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a12","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a13","reference":"40:11"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a14","text":")."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"aw","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/7"}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a16","tone":"primary","style":"rule","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a18","style":"bodySmItalic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":"The following page is a reproduction of the lead article from the February, 1990 issue of the Submitters Perspective, the monthly bulletin of United Submitters International. This issue was completed and mailed ahead of time, in December, 1989. Dr. Khalifa was martyred on January 31, 1990 and his soul was taken directly to Paradise."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a19","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1a","text":"New Major Revelation: Great News for the Believers — The Righteous Do Not Really Die, They Go Straight to Heaven"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a1e","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1b","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1c","text":"“Give good news to those who believe and work righteousness that they will have gardens with flowing streams. When provided with provisions of fruits therein, they will say, ‘This is what was given to us in the past.’ They will be given similar provisions, and they will have pure spouses therein. They abide therein forever.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1d","reference":"2:25"}]}}]},{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a1i","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1f","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1g","text":"“Do not think that those who are killed in the cause of God are dead; they are alive at their Lord, being provided for.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1h","reference":"3:169"}]}}]},{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a1m","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1j","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1k","text":"“Do NOT say about those who are killed in the cause of God, ‘They are dead’ For they are alive, but you do not perceive.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1l","reference":"2:154"}]}}]},{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a1q","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1n","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1o","text":"“O you who believe, you shall respond to God and the messenger when he invites to what keeps you alive.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1p","reference":"8:24"}]}}]},{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a1u","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1r","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1s","text":"“Those who emigrate in the cause of God, then get killed or die, God will surely provide for them a good provision.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1t","reference":"22:58"}]}}]},{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a1y","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1v","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1w","text":"“They do not taste death, beyond the first death, and God spares them the retribution of Hell.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1x","reference":"44:56"}]}}]},{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a24","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1z","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a20","text":"“He was told, ‘Enter Paradise.’ He said, ‘I wish my people (on earth) know, that my Lord has forgiven me and honored me.’”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a21","reference":"36:26"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a22","text":"–"},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a23","reference":"36:27"}]}}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a25","tone":"muted","style":"mutedCentered","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a27","style":"bodySmItalic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a26","text":"The wages of sin is death."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a29","style":"noteMonoSpaced","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a28","text":"[Romans 6:23]"}]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a2j","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2a","text":"As stated in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2b","reference":"3:81"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2c","text":" and "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2d","reference":"46:9"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2e","text":", God’s Messenger of the Covenant does not bring anything new; everything I receive and pass on to you is already in the Quran. However, the Quran is full of information that is kept by Almighty God for revelation at a specific time. Now is the time to look at the verses shown above and learn the great news: THE RIGHTEOUS DO NOT DIE; when their lives on this earth come to the predetermined end, the angel of death simply invites them to leave their earthly bodies and move on to Heaven, the same Paradise where Adam and Eve once lived. Heaven has been in existence since Adam and Eve. We learn from "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2f","reference":"89:27"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2g","text":"–"},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2h","reference":"89:30"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2i","text":" that God invites the believers’ souls: “Enter My Paradise.”"}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a2k","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2l","text":"My Own Experience"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"block","_key":"a2r","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2m","text":"When God’s covenant with the prophets was fulfilled in accordance with "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2n","reference":"3:81"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2o","text":", I was taken to Heaven where the righteous live NOW ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2p","reference":"4:69"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2q","text":"). While my body was down here on earth, I was in the same Paradise of Adam & Eve."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a2s","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2t","text":"The Disbelievers"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"block","_key":"a3d","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2u","text":"As for the disbelievers, they know at the moment of death that they are destined for Hell. The angels beat them up on the faces and rear ends ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2v","reference":"8:50"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2w","text":" & "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2x","reference":"47:27"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2y","text":"), order them to evict their souls ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2z","reference":"6:93"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a30","text":"), then “snatch their souls” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a31","reference":"79:1"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a32","text":"). The Quran teaches that the disbelievers go through 2 deaths ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a33","reference":"2:28"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a34","text":" & "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a35","reference":"40:11"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a36","text":"). They will be put to death — a state of nothingness during which they see Hell day and night in a continuous nightmare that lasts until the Day of Judgment ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a37","reference":"40:46"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a38","text":"). Hell is not yet in existence ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a39","reference":"40:46"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3a","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3b","reference":"89:23"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3c","text":")."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a3e","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3f","text":"Of Course, the Righteous Depart"}]},"centered":true},{"_type":"block","_key":"a3n","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3g","text":"As far as people are concerned, the righteous “die.” People do not realize that the righteous simply leave their bodies, and move on to Paradise. The verses shown above are self explanatory. They tell us that the righteous die only once — the one death we have already experienced as a consequence of the great fued ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3h","reference":"38:69"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3i","text":"). In "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3j","reference":"36:26"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3k","text":"–"},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3l","reference":"36:27"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3m","text":", we see the best evidence that the righteous go to Paradise, while their friends and relatives are still living on earth. Like going to Hawaii and waiting for us there."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a3v","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3o","text":"See also "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3p","reference":"16:32"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3q","text":" & "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3r","reference":"6:60"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3s","text":"–"},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3t","reference":"6:62"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3u","text":"."}]}]$pt$::jsonb, 'UW0MnqokXvY', 'Appendix 17 — Death'),

-- Appendix 18: Quran is All You Need
('18', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a4","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“We did not leave anything out of this book.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"6:38"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ac","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"The words of the Quran speak in "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"19:64"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":", saying, “We come down in accordance with the commandments of your Lord. To Him belongs the past, present, and the future. Your Lord never forgets.” God did not forget, for example, to tell us how to sleep ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8","reference":"18:109"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"31:27"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":"). Yet, the fabricators of such false doctrine as Hadith & Sunna have come up with religious teachings dictating on their followers how to sleep, and even how to cut your nails. The Sacred Mosque in Mecca and the illegal “Sacred Mosque” of Medina, hire some individuals to seek out the exhausted visitors and beat them with a stick if they fall asleep on the wrong side!"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"as","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":"The Quran proclaims that the Quran is complete, perfect, and fully detailed ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ae","reference":"6:19"},{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ag","reference":"6:38"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ai","reference":"6:114"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aj","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ak","reference":"6:115"},{"_type":"span","_key":"al","text":"; "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"am","reference":"50:45"},{"_type":"span","_key":"an","text":"), and that religious regulations not specifically instituted in the Quran constitute a religion other than Islam ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ao","reference":"42:21"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ap","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aq","reference":"17:46"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ar","text":"). The true believers uphold the Quran, the whole Quran, and nothing but the Quran."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1a","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"at","text":"This principle is confirmed by the Quran's mathematical code. Verse 46 of Sura 17 proclaims that we shall uphold the Quran ALONE. The word “ALONE” occurs in the Quran 6 times: "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"au","reference":"7:70"},{"_type":"span","_key":"av","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aw","reference":"17:46"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ay","reference":"39:45"},{"_type":"span","_key":"az","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a10","reference":"40:12"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a11","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a12","reference":"40:84"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a13","text":", and "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a14","reference":"60:4"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":". All these occurrences refer to God, except "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a16","reference":"17:46"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":". When we add the numbers of suras and verses which refer to “GOD ALONE,” we get 361, 19x19. This proves that "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a18","reference":"17:46"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a19","text":" refers to “the Quran ALONE.”"}]}]$pt$::jsonb, 'kc6zPAeY83Q', 'Appendix 18 — Quran: All You Need For Salvation'),

-- Appendix 19: Hadith and Sunna: Satanic Innovations
('19', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"aj","style":"stacked","body":"sm","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“Which Hadith, other than God and His revelations, do they uphold?”"}]},"wrapper":"grouped","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"45:6"}]}},{"_key":"a4","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"“The Quran is not a fabricated Hadith; ...it details everything.”"}]},"wrapper":"grouped","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"12:111"}]}},{"_key":"a7","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a8","text":"“Some people uphold vain Hadith to divert others from the path of God.”"}]},"wrapper":"grouped","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a9","reference":"31:6"}]}},{"_key":"aa","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":"“The only Sunna to follow shall be God's Sunna.”"}]},"wrapper":"grouped","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac","reference":"17:77"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ae","reference":"33:62"},{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ag","reference":"48:23"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ai","reference":"6:114"}]}}]},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a27","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"ax","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ak","text":"The Quran informs us that some enemies of the Prophet, described as “human and jinn devils,” will fabricate lies and attribute them to the Prophet ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"al","reference":"6:112"},{"_type":"span","_key":"am","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"an","reference":"25:31"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ao","text":"). This is precisely what happened after the prophet Muhammad’s death; "},{"_type":"span","_key":"ap","text":"Hadith","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"aq","text":" (oral) and "},{"_type":"span","_key":"ar","text":"Sunna","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"as","text":" (actions) were invented and attributed to the Prophet. "},{"_type":"span","_key":"at","text":"Hadith","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"au","text":" and "},{"_type":"span","_key":"av","text":"Sunna","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"aw","text":" are satanic innovations because they:"}]},{"_type":"appendixBadgeList","_key":"a1c","ordered":false,"style":"badge","item":"badge","marker":"primary","content":"sm","contentTag":"span","items":[{"_key":"ay","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"az","text":"defy the divine assertions that the Quran is complete, perfect, fully detailed, and shall be the only source of religious guidance ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a10","reference":"6:19"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a11","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a12","reference":"6:38"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a13","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a14","reference":"6:114"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":" & "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a16","reference":"45:6"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":"-7),"}]}},{"_key":"a18","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a19","text":"blaspheme against the Prophet and depict him as a vicious tyrant who did not uphold the Quran, and"}]}},{"_key":"a1a","text":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1b","text":"create false doctrines based on superstition, ignorance, and indefensible nonsensical traditions."}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1g","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1d","text":"The prophet Muhammad was enjoined, in very strong words, from issuing any religious teachings besides the Quran ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1e","reference":"69:38"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1f","text":"-48)."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1r","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1h","text":"Some Muslims compromise: “If a "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1i","text":"Hadith","marks":["em"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1j","text":" agrees with the Quran we will accept it, and if it contradicts the Quran, we will reject it!” Such premise proves that these people do not believe God’s assertions that the Quran is “complete, perfect and fully detailed.” The moment they seek guidance from anything besides the Quran, no matter how “right” it seems, they fall into Satan’s trap (see "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1k","reference":"63:1"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1l","text":"). For they have rejected God’s word and set up another god besides God ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1m","reference":"18:57"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1n","text":"). See "},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p","text":"Appendix 33","marks":["a1o"]},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1q","text":"."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"a1o","_type":"link","href":"/appendices/33"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1t","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1s","text":"The Quran’s mathematical miracle provides mathematical evidence that the Quran shall be our only source of religious teachings. Here are just 2 examples:"}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1u","tone":"neutral","style":"outline","content":[{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a20","style":"stackFlush","tag":"div","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1x","style":"term","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1v","text":"1. "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1w","reference":"6:38"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1z","style":"bodyRelaxed","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1y","text":"“We did not leave anything out of this book,” is in Verse 38 (19x2) and consists of 19 Arabic letters."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a26","style":"stackFlush","tag":"div","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a23","style":"term","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21","text":"2. "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a22","reference":"6:114"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a25","style":"bodyRelaxed","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a24","text":"“He sent down this book fully detailed,” is in Verse 114 (19x6) and consists of 19 Arabic letters."}]}]}]}]}]$pt$::jsonb, '2UxqrtHtYd0', 'Appendix 19 — Hadith & Sunna: Satanic Innovations'),

-- Appendix 20: Quran: Unlike Any Other Book
('20', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a4","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“The Most Gracious. Teacher of the Quran.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"55:1-2"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ae","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":"The Quran is God's Final Testament to the world, and He has pledged to protect it from the slightest distortion ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"15:9"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":"). Thus, the Quran is surrounded by invisible forces that guard it and serve it ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8","reference":"13:39"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"41:42"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac","reference":"42:24"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":")."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ai","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":"Unlike any other book, the Quran is taught by God ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ag","reference":"55:1"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":"-2); He teaches us what we need at the time we need it. This is why we read the Quran hundreds of times without getting bored. We can read a novel, for example, only once. But the Quran can be read an infinite number of times, and we derive new and valuable information from it every time."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"as","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aj","text":"On the other hand, the insincere readers - those who read the Quran to find fault with it - are diverted from the Quran ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ak","reference":"7:146"},{"_type":"span","_key":"al","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"am","reference":"17:45"},{"_type":"span","_key":"an","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ao","reference":"18:57"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ap","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aq","reference":"41:44"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ar","text":"). In fact, God's invisible forces help them find the faults they seek. Since the Quran is perfect, such “faults” serve only to reveal the stupidity of God's enemies."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"at","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"au","text":"God's Attributes for the Quran"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"a14","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"av","text":"God uses His own attributes to describe the Quran; He calls the Quran “ʿAzeem = Great” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aw","reference":"15:87"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":"), “Hakeem = Full of wisdom” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ay","reference":"36:2"},{"_type":"span","_key":"az","text":"), “Majid = Glorious” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a10","reference":"50:1"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a11","text":"), and “Karim = Honorable” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a12","reference":"56:77"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a13","text":"). What can we say?"}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a15","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a16","text":"Accessible to All Believers"}]}},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1a","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":"Since the Quran is God's message to all the people, regardless of their language, the Quran is accessible to the believers, regardless of their language ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a18","reference":"41:44"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a19","text":"). This explains a profound phenomenon: the believers who do not know Arabic know the Quran better than the Arabic speaking unbelievers."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1i","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1b","text":"Because of the invisible forces serving the Quran, it is readily and enjoyably accessible to the sincere believers, and utterly inaccessible to the unbelievers ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1c","reference":"17:45"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1d","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1e","reference":"18:57"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1f","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a1g","reference":"56:79"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a1h","text":")."}]}]$pt$::jsonb, 'vQwdGHGu570', 'Appendix 20 — Quran: Unlike Any Other Book'),

-- Appendix 21: Satan: Fallen Angel
('21', $pt$[{"_type":"block","_key":"a2","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1","text":"In God’s kingdom, certain creatures are necessarily given the powers needed to perform their duties. Satan believed that his God-given powers qualified him to function as an independent god. As evidenced by the prevalence of misery, disease, accidents, and war in his dominion, we now know that Satan is incompetent."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"am","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3","text":"The Quran clearly states that Satan was an angel, by virtue of the immense powers and rank bestowed upon him. This is why he is addressed as an angel ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a4","reference":"2:34"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a5","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a6","reference":"7:11"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a7","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a8","reference":"15:29"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"17:61"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac","reference":"18:50"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ae","reference":"20:116"},{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ag","reference":"38:71"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":") prior to his fall. By definition, a jinn is a fallen angel ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ai","reference":"18:50"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aj","text":"). Satan’s rebellion teaches us that the angels were created with minds of their own, and absolute freedom of choice ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ak","reference":"2:34"},{"_type":"span","_key":"al","text":")."}]}]$pt$::jsonb, 'NdMD2geQbdc', 'Appendix 21 — Satan: Fallen Angel'),

-- Appendix 22: Jesus
('22', $pt$[{"_type":"appendixVerseCards","_key":"a8","style":"centerTight","body":"base","refStyle":"plain","entries":[{"_key":"a1","body":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2","text":"“God is my Lord and your Lord; you shall worship Him alone. This is the right path.”"}]},"wrapper":"none","reference":{"children":[{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3","reference":"3:51"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a4","text":" · "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a5","reference":"19:36"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a6","text":" · "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a7","reference":"43:64"}]}}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"am","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a9","text":"The Quran, informs us that Jesus was a human messenger of God whose sole mission was to deliver God's message; he never possessed any power, and is now dead ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"aa","reference":"4:171"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ab","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ac","reference":"5:75"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ad","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ae","reference":"5:117"},{"_type":"span","_key":"af","text":"). Those who consider Jesus to be God, or Son of God, or part of a trinity are “pagans” ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ag","reference":"5:17"},{"_type":"span","_key":"ah","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ai","reference":"5:72"},{"_type":"span","_key":"aj","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"ak","reference":"5:73"},{"_type":"span","_key":"al","text":"). Outstanding Christian scholars have reached these same conclusions (THE MYTH OF GOD INCARNATE, John Hick, ed., The Westminster Press, Philadelphia, 1977 & THE MYTH MAKER, Hyam Maccoby, Harper & Row 1986). Christianity is the product of Nicene (AD 325)."}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"an","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ao","text":"The Bible's Jesus"}]}},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a2s","style":"proseSm","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"ap","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"ar","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"aq","text":"Jesus proclaimed aloud: “Whoever puts faith in me believes not so much in me as in him who sent me; ..... For I have not spoken on my own; no, the Father who sent me has commanded me what to say and how to speak. Since I know that his commandment means eternal life, whatever I say is spoken just as he instructed me.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"at","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"as","text":"[John 12:44-50]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"au","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"aw","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"av","text":"“I cannot do anything of myself. I judge as I hear, and my judgment is honest because I am not seeking my own will but the will of him who sent me.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"ay","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"ax","text":"[John 5:30]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"az","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a11","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a10","text":"Jesus said: “My doctrine is not my own; it comes from him who sent me.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a13","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a12","text":"[John 7:16]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a14","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a16","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a15","text":"“Men of Israel, listen to me! Jesus the Nazorean was a man whom God sent to you with miracles, wonders, and signs as his credentials. These God worked through him in your midst, as you well know.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a18","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a17","text":"[Acts 2:22]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a19","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1b","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1a","text":"“...The man who hears my word and has faith in him who sent me possesses eternal life.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1d","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1c","text":"[John 5:24]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1e","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1g","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1f","text":"“Whoever welcomes me welcomes, not me, but him who sent me.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1i","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1h","text":"[Mattew 10:40, Mark 9:37, Luke 9:48, & John 13:20]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1j","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1l","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1k","text":"“...I have not come of myself. I was sent by One who has the right to send, and him you do not know. I know him because it is from him I come; he sent me.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1n","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1m","text":"[John 7:28-29]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1o","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1q","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1p","text":"Jesus looked up to heaven and said, “...Eternal life is this: to know you, the only true God, and him whom you have sent, Jesus Christ.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1s","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1r","text":"[John 17:1-3]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1t","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a1v","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1u","text":"All who are led by the Spirit of God are sons of God."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a1x","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1w","text":"[Romans 8:14]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a1y","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a20","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a1z","text":"Jesus looked upward and said, “Father, I thank you for having heard me. I know that you always hear me but I have said this for the sake of the crowd, that they may believe that you sent me.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a22","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a21","text":"[John 11:41-42]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a23","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a25","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a24","text":"As he was setting out on a journey a man came running up, knelt down before him and asked, “Good Teacher, what must I do to share in everlasting life?” Jesus answered, “Why do you call me good? No one is good but God alone.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a27","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a26","text":"[Mark 10:17-18]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a28","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a2a","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a29","text":"“None of those who call me ‘Lord’ will enter the kingdom of God, but only the one who does the will of my Father in heaven.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a2c","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2b","text":"[Matthew 7:21]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a2d","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a2f","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2e","text":"“...Go to my brothers and tell them, ‘I am ascending to my Father and your Father, to my God and your God.’ ”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a2h","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2g","text":"[John 20:17]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a2i","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a2k","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2j","text":"“God is my Lord and your Lord; you shall worship Him alone. This is the right path.”"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a2m","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2l","text":"[Quran 3:51, 19:36, & 43:64]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a2n","tone":"primary","style":"ruleTight","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a2p","style":"italic","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2o","text":"Trinity, the doctrine of God taught by Christians that asserts that God is one in essence but three in “person,” Father, Son, and Holy Spirit. Neither the word Trinity, nor the explicit doctrine as such, appears in the New Testament, nor did Jesus and his followers intend to contradict the Shema in the Old Testament: “Hear O Israel: The Lord our God is one” (Deut. 6:4)."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a2r","style":"noteMono","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2q","text":"[Encyclopaedia Britannica, 1975]"}]}],"reveal":false}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a2t","tone":"primary","style":"example","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a2z","style":"medium","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a2u","text":"Jesus' soul was raised, i.e., he was put to death prior to the arrest and crucifixion of his body. Thus, his persecutors arrested, tortured, and crucified an empty body - Jesus was already gone to the world of souls ("},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2v","reference":"3:55"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2w","text":", "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a2x","reference":"4:157"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a2y","text":")."}]}]},{"_type":"appendixDivider","_key":"a30","title":{"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a31","text":"Jesus' Death"}]}},{"_type":"appendixSection","_key":"a3s","style":"prose","tag":"section","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a33","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a32","text":"This has been the single most controversial subject in the world. The Quran's miraculous mathematical code has now provided the final answer to this topic:"}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a34","tone":"primary","style":"ruleStack","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a38","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a35","text":"They plotted and schemed, but so did God, and God is the best schemer. Thus, God said, “O Jesus, I am putting you to death, and raising you to Me; I will save you from the disbelievers.” [Quran "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a36","reference":"3:54"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a37","text":"-55]"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a3c","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a39","text":"They claimed that they killed the Messiah, Jesus, the son of Mary, the messenger of God! In fact, they never killed him; they never crucified him; they were led to believe that they did. [Quran "},{"_type":"quranRef","_key":"a3a","reference":"4:157"},{"_type":"span","_key":"a3b","text":"]"}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a3e","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3d","text":"Mercifully, God has given our generation a living example of a person whose soul departed this world, but his body continued to live for 19 months. On November 25, 1984, doctors at the Humana Hospital of Louisville, Kentucky removed the diseased heart of Mr. William Schroeder and replaced it with a plastic and metal pump (THE NEW YORK TIMES, Monday, November 26, 1984)."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a3g","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3f","text":"On the 19th day after this historic operation - Thursday, December 13, 1984 - Mr. Schroeder, the soul, the real person, departed this world. Mr. Schroeder died. But his body continued to function with the artificial heart implanted in his body. The world was told that he “probably suffered a stroke” (THE NEW YORK TIMES, December 14, 1984). Significantly, only one day before Mr. Schroeder's departure, he talked with President Ronald Reagan on national TV, and demanded that the Social Security Administration send his overdue check. He was perfectly alert. From the moment “he suffered a stroke,” he did not recognize the day or time, nor his family members. In fact, Mr. Scroeder was no longer in this world."}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a3i","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3h","text":"The Gospels state clearly that the arrested body of Jesus was oblivious to the events surrounding it:"}]},{"_type":"appendixCallout","_key":"a3j","tone":"primary","style":"ruleStack","content":[{"_type":"block","_key":"a3l","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3k","text":"The chief priests, meanwhile, brought many accusations against Jesus. Pilate interrogated him again: “Surely you have some answer? See how many accusations they are leveling against you.” But greatly to Pilate's surprise, Jesus made no further response. [Mark 15:3-5]"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a3n","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3m","text":"Herod was extremely pleased to see Jesus. From the reports about him he had wanted for a long time to see him, and he was hoping to see him work some miracles. He questioned Jesus at considerable length, but Jesus made no response. The chief priests and scribes were at hand to accuse him vehemently. Herod and his guards then treated him with contempt and insult. [Luke 23:8-11]"}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"a3p","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3o","text":"The Savior said to me, “He whom you saw on the tree, glad and laughing, this is the living Jesus. But this one into whose hands and feet they drive the nails is the fleshly part. [Apocalypse of Peter, VII, 3, 81] from THE NAG HAMMADI LIBRARY (Harper & Row, 1977, James M. Robinson, ed, Page 339)."}]}],"reveal":false},{"_type":"block","_key":"a3r","style":"normal","children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"a3q","text":"The facts that (1) Mr. Schroeder's soul departed on the 19th day after the operation, and (2) his body survived for 19 months, are uncanny reminders that God wanted the world to know the parallel between Schroeder's situation, and the proven account of Jesus' departure prior to the arrest, torture, and crucifixion of his soulless body."}]}]}]$pt$::jsonb, 'seAyTYQrsPM', 'Appendix 22 — Jesus'),

